/* AI brain Q&A pack 27: arts, music, books & myths */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["what is a musical scale | what is a scale in music | music scales explained", "A **scale** is a set of notes played in order going up or down, like a musical staircase. The most familiar one is the major scale: do, re, mi, fa, sol, la, ti, do. Melodies and chords are built from scales."],
  ["what is a major scale | major scale pattern | how to build a major scale", "A **major scale** follows the step pattern **whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half**. Starting on C, that gives C D E F G A B C, which is all white keys on a piano. Major scales usually sound bright and happy."],
  ["what is a minor scale | natural minor scale | how to build a minor scale", "The **natural minor** scale uses the step pattern **whole, half, whole, whole, half, whole, whole**. A minor (A B C D E F G A) uses only the white keys. Minor scales often sound sadder, darker or more mysterious than major ones."],
  ["why do minor keys sound sad | why does minor music sound sad", "A minor key has a lowered third note, which makes its home chord sound darker than a major chord. Part of the effect is the sound itself and part is habit, because we've heard minor music in so many sad or spooky scenes. Minor music can also sound serious, exciting or heroic, not only sad."],
  ["what is the harmonic minor scale | harmonic minor vs melodic minor", "The **harmonic minor** scale is a natural minor scale with the 7th note raised a half step, which gives it a dramatic, exotic sound. The **melodic minor** raises both the 6th and 7th notes on the way up and traditionally goes back to the natural minor on the way down."],
  ["what is a chromatic scale | chromatic scale meaning", "A **chromatic scale** uses all 12 notes, moving up or down one half step at a time. On a piano, that means playing every white and black key in a row."],
  ["what is a pentatonic scale | pentatonic scale meaning | 5 note scale", "A **pentatonic scale** has just five notes in each octave. The black keys of a piano form one, which is why it's hard to sound \"wrong\" when you play only black keys! Pentatonic scales are found in folk music all over the world and in blues and rock solos."],
  ["what is the blues scale | how to play the blues scale", "The **blues scale** is a minor pentatonic scale plus one extra \"blue note,\" the flattened fifth. In A it goes A, C, D, E♭, E, G. It gives the soulful, gritty sound you hear in blues, rock and jazz solos."],
  ["what are modes in music | what is dorian mode | what are the musical modes", "**Modes** are scales you get by starting a major scale on a different note. The seven modes are Ionian (major), Dorian, Phrygian, Lydian, Mixolydian, Aeolian (natural minor) and Locrian. Each has its own mood: Dorian sounds cool and jazzy, Lydian dreamy and Mixolydian bluesy."],
  ["what is a whole step | what is a half step | whole tone vs semitone", "A **half step** (semitone) is the smallest distance in Western music, from one piano key to the very next key, black or white. A **whole step** (whole tone) equals two half steps, like C to D."],
  ["what is a key in music | what does it mean when a song is in a key", "The **key** of a piece tells you its home note and the scale it mostly uses. A song in G major uses the notes of the G major scale and feels \"finished\" when it lands on G."],
  ["what is a key change in a song | what is modulation in music", "A **key change** (modulation) moves the music to a new home note partway through. Pop songs often jump up a step for the final chorus to add a burst of energy, and classical pieces change key to create contrast and drama."],
  ["what is a key signature | how to read a key signature", "A **key signature** is the group of sharps or flats written at the start of every line of sheet music. It tells you which notes to play sharp or flat all the way through, so they don't need marking each time. For example, one sharp (F♯) means G major or E minor."],
  ["what is a sharp in music | what does a sharp sign mean", "A **sharp** (♯) raises a note by one half step. F♯ is the key just above F, which on a piano is a black key."],
  ["what is a flat in music | what does a flat sign mean", "A **flat** (♭) lowers a note by one half step. B♭ is the key just below B, which on a piano is a black key."],
  ["what is a natural sign in music | what does a natural mean in music", "A **natural** sign (♮) cancels a sharp or flat, so you play the plain version of the note. For example, F♮ in a G major piece means play F instead of F♯."],
  ["what is an accidental in music | accidentals meaning", "An **accidental** is a sharp, flat or natural written right in front of a note that isn't in the key signature. It usually lasts until the end of that measure."],
  ["what are enharmonic notes | why is c sharp the same as d flat", "**Enharmonic** notes sound exactly the same but are written with different names, like C♯ and D♭. On a piano they're the same black key. Which name you use depends on the key and the musical context."],
  ["how many keys are there in music | how many major and minor keys are there", "There are **12 major keys and 12 minor keys**, one for each of the 12 notes. Because a few can be spelled two ways (like F♯ major and G♭ major), you'll often see 15 key signatures listed for each."],
  ["what is the circle of fifths | how does the circle of fifths work", "The **circle of fifths** arranges the 12 keys in a circle, each a fifth apart: C, G, D, A, E, B, F♯ and so on. Going clockwise adds one sharp at each step, and going counterclockwise (C, F, B♭, E♭…) adds one flat. Musicians use it to find key signatures and chords that sound good together."],
  ["what is the order of sharps | order of sharps mnemonic", "Sharps always appear in this order: **F C G D A E B**. A popular memory trick is \"Father Charles Goes Down And Ends Battle.\""],
  ["what is the order of flats | order of flats mnemonic", "Flats are the order of sharps backwards: **B E A D G C F**. Remember it as \"BEAD\" plus \"Greatest Common Factor,\" or \"Battle Ends And Down Goes Charles' Father.\""],
  ["what is a relative minor | relative major and minor", "Every major key has a **relative minor** that shares exactly the same key signature. It starts on the 6th note of the major scale, so C major and A minor both have no sharps or flats."],
  ["what is a time signature | how to read a time signature", "A **time signature** is the pair of stacked numbers at the start of a piece. The top number tells how many beats are in each measure, and the bottom number tells which note gets one beat (4 means a quarter note, 8 means an eighth note)."],
  ["what does 4/4 time mean | what is common time", "**4/4** means four quarter-note beats in every measure: count 1, 2, 3, 4. It's used so much in pop, rock and classical music that it's nicknamed **common time** and is sometimes written as a big \"C.\""],
  ["what does 3/4 time mean | what is waltz time", "**3/4** has three quarter-note beats in each measure, counted ONE, two, three. It gives a swaying feel and is the time signature of the waltz."],
  ["what does 6/8 time mean | what is compound time", "**6/8** has six eighth notes per measure, usually felt as two big beats each split into three: ONE-two-three, FOUR-five-six. Time signatures where the beat divides into threes are called **compound time**."],
  ["what does 2/4 time mean | what is cut time", "**2/4** has two quarter-note beats per measure and is great for marches and polkas. **Cut time** (2/2, written as a \"C\" with a line through it) has two half-note beats per measure."],
  ["what is an odd time signature | what is 5/4 time | what is 7/8 time", "Odd time signatures like **5/4** or **7/8** have beats that don't group evenly into twos or threes, which gives a lopsided, interesting groove. Dave Brubeck's jazz tune \"Take Five\" is a famous piece in 5/4."],
  ["what is a measure in music | what is a bar in music | what is a bar line", "A **measure** (also called a **bar**) is a small section of music between two vertical **bar lines**. Each measure holds the number of beats the time signature says."],
  ["what are note values | types of musical notes | how long is each note", "Note values tell you how long to hold a note. In 4/4 time a **whole note** lasts 4 beats, a **half note** 2 beats, a **quarter note** 1 beat, an **eighth note** half a beat and a **sixteenth note** a quarter of a beat. Each one is half as long as the one before."],
  ["what is a whole note | what does a whole note look like", "A **whole note** looks like a hollow oval with no stem. In 4/4 time it lasts four beats, filling a whole measure."],
  ["what is a half note | how many beats is a half note", "A **half note** is a hollow oval with a stem. In 4/4 time it lasts two beats."],
  ["what is a quarter note | how many beats is a quarter note", "A **quarter note** is a filled-in oval with a stem, and it usually gets one beat. It's one of the most common notes in music."],
  ["what is an eighth note | what is a sixteenth note", "An **eighth note** is a filled oval with a stem and one flag (or a beam joining it to its neighbors), and it lasts half a beat in 4/4 time. A **sixteenth note** has two flags or beams and lasts a quarter of a beat."],
  ["what is a dotted note | what does a dot after a note mean", "A dot after a note adds **half of its value** to it. So a dotted half note lasts 2 + 1 = 3 beats, and a dotted quarter note lasts 1½ beats."],
  ["what is a tie in music | tie vs slur", "A **tie** is a curved line joining two notes of the same pitch: you play the note once and hold it for both lengths. A **slur** is a curved line over different notes, meaning you play them smoothly connected."],
  ["what is a rest in music | music rests explained", "A **rest** is a symbol for silence. Every note value has a matching rest (whole rest, half rest, quarter rest and so on) that tells you how long to stay quiet."],
  ["what is a triplet in music | how do you count triplets", "A **triplet** squeezes three equal notes into the time normally taken by two. It's marked with a small \"3\" over the group and gives a rolling, galloping feel. Many people count them as \"trip-a-let.\""],
  ["what is syncopation | syncopation meaning", "**Syncopation** puts accents on off-beats or between the beats, where you don't expect them. It makes music feel bouncy and lively and is a big part of jazz, funk, reggae and pop."],
  ["what is a clef | what does a clef do", "A **clef** is the symbol at the start of a staff that tells you which note each line and space stands for. The most common are the treble clef and the bass clef."],
  ["what is a treble clef | what is the g clef | treble clef notes", "The **treble clef** (G clef) curls around the second line, marking it as the G above middle C. It's used for higher sounds like the piano's right hand, violin, flute and most singing. The lines from bottom to top are E G B D F (\"Every Good Boy Does Fine\") and the spaces spell F A C E."],
  ["what is a bass clef | what is the f clef | bass clef notes", "The **bass clef** (F clef) has two dots around the fourth line, marking it as the F below middle C. It's used for low sounds like cello, tuba and the piano's left hand. The lines from bottom to top are G B D F A (\"Good Boys Do Fine Always\") and the spaces are A C E G (\"All Cows Eat Grass\")."],
  ["what is an alto clef | what is a c clef | what clef does viola use", "The **alto clef** is a C clef whose center points to middle C on the middle line of the staff. It's mainly used by the **viola**. The tenor clef, another C clef, is used for high cello, bassoon and trombone parts."],
  ["what is a music staff | what is a stave | what are ledger lines", "The **staff** (or stave) is the set of five lines and four spaces where notes are written; the higher a note sits, the higher it sounds. Short extra lines above or below the staff for very high or low notes are called **ledger lines**."],
  ["what is middle c | where is middle c on the piano", "**Middle C** is the C closest to the center of the piano keyboard, usually right under the brand name. On sheet music it sits on a ledger line between the treble and bass staves."],
  ["what is a grand staff | what is the piano staff", "The **grand staff** is a treble staff and a bass staff joined by a brace, used for piano and harp music. Usually the right hand reads the top staff and the left hand reads the bottom."],
  ["what is solfege | do re mi meaning | what are solfege syllables", "**Solfège** gives each note of the scale a singable name: **do, re, mi, fa, sol, la, ti, do**. It helps singers learn to hear and sing pitches. The song \"Do-Re-Mi\" from The Sound of Music teaches it."],
  ["what is an interval in music | music intervals explained", "An **interval** is the distance between two notes. Intervals are named by counting letter names, so C to E is a **third**, C to G is a **fifth** and C to the next C is an **octave**. They can also be called major, minor, perfect, augmented or diminished."],
  ["what is a perfect fifth | what is a perfect fourth", "A **perfect fifth** spans seven half steps (C to G) and sounds open and strong, like the jump between the first two \"twinkles\" in \"Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star.\" A **perfect fourth** spans five half steps (C to F), like the start of \"Here Comes the Bride.\""],
  ["what is a major third | major third vs minor third", "A **major third** spans four half steps (C to E) and sounds bright. A **minor third** spans three half steps (C to E♭) and sounds darker. Swapping one for the other is what turns a major chord into a minor chord."],
  ["what is a tritone | why is the tritone called the devil's interval", "A **tritone** is six half steps, like C to F♯, which is exactly half an octave. It sounds tense and unsettled, so centuries ago people nicknamed it \"the devil in music.\" Today composers use it on purpose to build suspense, and you can hear it at the start of \"Maria\" from West Side Story."],
  ["songs to remember intervals | how to recognize intervals by ear", "Many musicians link intervals to famous tunes: a **perfect fourth** starts \"Here Comes the Bride,\" a **perfect fifth** starts \"Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star,\" a **major sixth** starts \"My Bonnie Lies Over the Ocean\" and an **octave** starts \"Somewhere Over the Rainbow.\" A **major third** opens \"Oh When the Saints.\""],
  ["what is ear training | how to train your ear for music", "**Ear training** means practicing to recognize notes, intervals, chords and rhythms just by listening. Try singing scales, matching notes on an instrument, naming intervals with song tricks and copying short melodies by ear. A few minutes a day adds up fast."],
  ["what is perfect pitch | what is absolute pitch | can you learn perfect pitch", "**Perfect pitch** (absolute pitch) is the rare ability to name or sing any note without hearing a reference note first. It's very uncommon and usually develops in early childhood, so it's hard to learn later. Most great musicians rely on relative pitch instead."],
  ["what is relative pitch | relative pitch vs perfect pitch", "**Relative pitch** is the skill of recognizing notes by how far apart they are from each other, like hearing that a melody jumps up a fifth. Unlike perfect pitch, anyone can develop it with ear training, and it's what most musicians use every day."],
  ["what is a triad | how are chords built", "A **triad** is a three-note chord built by stacking thirds: a root, a third and a fifth. C–E–G is a C major triad. Most chords in Western music are triads or are built from them."],
  ["what is a major chord | how to make a major chord", "A **major chord** uses a root, the note four half steps above it (a major third) and the note seven half steps above it (a perfect fifth). C major is C–E–G. Major chords sound bright and settled."],
  ["what is a minor chord | how to make a minor chord", "A **minor chord** uses a root, the note three half steps above it (a minor third) and a perfect fifth. A minor is A–C–E. Lowering only the middle note of a major chord turns it minor."],
  ["what is a diminished chord | what is an augmented chord", "A **diminished** chord stacks two minor thirds (like B–D–F) and sounds tense and spooky. An **augmented** chord stacks two major thirds (like C–E–G♯) and sounds dreamy and unfinished. Both like to move on to another chord."],
  ["what is a seventh chord | what is a dominant seventh chord", "A **seventh chord** adds a fourth note, a seventh above the root, to a triad. The **dominant seventh** (like G–B–D–F) sounds as if it really wants to move to the home chord, so it's used in everything from Bach to the blues."],
  ["what is a sus chord | what is a suspended chord", "A **suspended chord** replaces the third with the second (sus2) or the fourth (sus4), like Csus4 = C–F–G. Without a third it's neither major nor minor, so it sounds open, floaty or like it's waiting to resolve."],
  ["what is a power chord | how to play a power chord", "A **power chord** is just the root and the fifth, often with the octave added, like E5 = E–B–E. Without a third it isn't major or minor, so it stays clear and punchy with distortion, which makes it a favorite of rock and punk guitarists."],
  ["what is an arpeggio | arpeggio meaning", "An **arpeggio** is a chord played one note at a time instead of all at once, like C–E–G–C rolling upward. The word comes from the Italian for \"playing like a harp.\""],
  ["what is a chord progression | common chord progressions", "A **chord progression** is a series of chords played in order, forming the backbone of a song. Musicians label chords with Roman numerals: in C major, I = C, IV = F, V = G and vi = A minor. Famous progressions include I–IV–V, I–V–vi–IV and ii–V–I."],
  ["what is the 1 4 5 chord progression | i iv v progression | three chord songs", "The **I–IV–V** progression uses the 1st, 4th and 5th chords of a key, which are C, F and G in C major. It's the heart of countless folk, blues, country and rock & roll songs, so learning just three chords lets you play hundreds of tunes."],
  ["what is the four chord song | i v vi iv progression | 1 5 6 4 chord progression", "**I–V–vi–IV** (C–G–Am–F in C major) is so common in pop music that comedians have performed dozens of famous songs back to back using only these four chords. It feels uplifting and familiar."],
  ["what is a 12 bar blues | 12 bar blues chord pattern", "The **12-bar blues** is a 12-measure chord pattern: four bars of I, two of IV, two of I, then one bar each of V, IV, I and V. It's the foundation of blues and early rock & roll. In A it goes A A A A | D D A A | E D A E."],
  ["what is a cadence in music | perfect cadence vs plagal cadence", "A **cadence** is a chord ending that works like punctuation. A **perfect (authentic) cadence**, V to I, sounds like a full stop. A **plagal cadence**, IV to I, is the \"Amen\" ending at the end of hymns, and a **half cadence** stops on V, like a comma or a question."],
  ["what is the tonic | what is the dominant in music | scale degree names", "Each note of a scale has a name: 1 **tonic**, 2 supertonic, 3 mediant, 4 **subdominant**, 5 **dominant**, 6 submediant and 7 **leading tone**. The tonic is \"home,\" and the dominant creates the strongest pull back home."],
  ["what is dissonance in music | consonant vs dissonant chords", "**Consonant** notes sound stable and restful together, like a major chord. **Dissonant** notes clash and sound tense, like two neighboring piano keys played at once. Music feels exciting when it moves from dissonance to consonance, building tension and then releasing it."],
  ["what are tempo markings | italian tempo terms | tempo words in music", "Composers often use Italian words for speed. From slowest to fastest, common ones are **largo** (very slow and broad), **adagio** (slow), **andante** (walking pace), **moderato** (moderate), **allegro** (fast and lively), **vivace** (lively and quick) and **presto** (very fast). **Prestissimo** means as fast as possible!"],
  ["what does allegro mean | allegro tempo", "**Allegro** means fast, lively and cheerful, roughly 120 to 160 beats per minute. The first movements of many symphonies and sonatas are marked allegro."],
  ["what does adagio mean | adagio tempo", "**Adagio** means slow and relaxed, at roughly 55 to 75 beats per minute. Adagio movements are often the gentle, emotional middle section of a longer piece."],
  ["what does andante mean | andante tempo", "**Andante** means \"walking,\" so it's a calm, easygoing pace of roughly 75 to 105 beats per minute. **Andantino** is usually a little quicker than andante."],
  ["what does presto mean | what does largo mean", "**Presto** means very fast, often faster than 170 beats per minute. **Largo** means very slow and broad, around 40 to 60 beats per minute. They're near opposite ends of the tempo scale."],
  ["what does moderato mean | what does vivace mean", "**Moderato** means at a moderate, medium speed. **Vivace** means lively and fast, a bit quicker than allegro."],
  ["what is a metronome | what does bpm mean in music", "A **metronome** is a device or app that clicks a steady beat to help you keep time. Its speed is set in **BPM**, beats per minute, so 60 BPM is one click every second."],
  ["what does ritardando mean | what does accelerando mean | what is rubato", "**Ritardando** (rit.) means gradually slow down, and **accelerando** (accel.) means gradually speed up. **Rubato**, Italian for \"stolen time,\" means stretching and squeezing the tempo freely for expression, which is common in Romantic piano music."],
  ["what is a fermata | what does the bird's eye symbol mean in music", "A **fermata** looks like an eye or an arch with a dot under it. It means hold the note longer than written, for as long as the performer or conductor chooses."],
  ["what are dynamics in music | dynamic markings | music volume symbols", "**Dynamics** tell you how loud or soft to play. From softest to loudest: **pp** pianissimo (very soft), **p** piano (soft), **mp** mezzo-piano (medium soft), **mf** mezzo-forte (medium loud), **f** forte (loud) and **ff** fortissimo (very loud)."],
  ["what does forte mean | what does piano mean in music", "In music, **forte** means loud and **piano** means soft. The piano's full name is actually **pianoforte**, \"soft-loud,\" because unlike the harpsichord before it, it could play both soft and loud depending on how hard you press the keys."],
  ["what is a crescendo | what is a decrescendo | what does diminuendo mean", "A **crescendo** means gradually get louder and is shown by a sideways \"<\" shape called a hairpin. A **decrescendo** or **diminuendo** means gradually get softer and is shown by \">\"."],
  ["what does sforzando mean | what does sfz mean", "**Sforzando** (sfz or sf) means play one note or chord with a sudden, strong accent. It's like a musical exclamation point."],
  ["what is staccato | what is legato | staccato vs legato", "**Staccato**, shown by a dot over or under a note, means play it short and detached. **Legato** means smooth and connected, with no gaps between notes, and is often shown with a slur."],
  ["what is an accent in music | what does marcato mean | what is tenuto", "An **accent** mark (>) means play that note louder or stronger than the others. **Marcato** (^) is an even stronger accent. **Tenuto** (a short line) means hold the note for its full value, sometimes with slight emphasis."],
  ["what is a repeat sign | what does da capo mean | what is d.c. al fine", "A **repeat sign** (two dots beside a double bar line) means go back and play that section again. **D.C.**, short for *da capo*, means \"from the head,\" so go back to the beginning. **D.C. al Fine** means go back to the start and stop at the word *Fine*."],
  ["what is a coda | what does dal segno mean", "A **coda** (Italian for \"tail\") is an ending section, marked by a circle with crosshairs. **D.S.** (*dal segno*) means go back to the sign 𝄋 and play from there, often followed by \"al Coda,\" meaning jump to the coda when you reach the coda sign."],
  ["what is a grace note | what is a trill in music", "A **grace note** is a tiny decorative note played very quickly just before a main note. A **trill** (marked \"tr\") means rapidly alternate between the written note and the note above it. Both are types of **ornaments**."],
  ["what is pitch in music | high and low notes", "**Pitch** is how high or low a sound is. It depends on how fast something vibrates: faster vibrations make higher pitches and slower vibrations make lower ones."],
  ["what is timbre | what is tone color in music", "**Timbre** (pronounced TAM-ber) is the special quality of a sound that lets you tell instruments apart, even when they play the same note at the same volume. It's why a flute and a trumpet sound different playing middle C."],
  ["what is a440 | what is concert pitch | what is a tuning fork", "**Concert pitch** is the agreed tuning standard, where the A above middle C vibrates **440 times per second** (A440). A **tuning fork** is a two-pronged metal tool that rings at one exact pitch when struck, so musicians can tune to it."],
  ["what is a round in music | what is a canon in music", "A **round** is a song where several groups sing the same melody but start at different times, so it overlaps with itself, like \"Row, Row, Row Your Boat\" or \"Frère Jacques.\" A **canon** is the more formal version of this idea, and Pachelbel's Canon is a famous example."],
  ["what is a verse and chorus | what is a bridge in a song | song structure explained", "Many songs follow a pattern. The **verse** tells the story and changes words each time, the **chorus** is the catchy part that repeats with the same words, and the **bridge** is a contrasting section near the end before the last chorus. Songs may also have an **intro** and an **outro**."],
  ["what is a hook in a song | what makes a song catchy", "A **hook** is the short, catchy bit of a song that grabs your attention and sticks in your memory, often in the chorus. It can be a melody, a lyric, a rhythm or an instrument riff."],
  ["what is a riff in music | what is a lick in music", "A **riff** is a short, repeated musical pattern that drives a song, often played on guitar or bass. A **lick** is a short musical phrase or trick, usually part of a solo, that players learn and reuse."],
  ["what is improvisation in music | what does improvise mean in music", "**Improvisation** is making up music on the spot instead of reading it. It's central to jazz and blues, where players take turns soloing over the song's chords. Classical musicians like Bach and Mozart were also famous improvisers."],
  ["how to read guitar tabs | what is guitar tab | how do tabs work", "**Guitar tab** (tablature) uses six lines for the six strings, with the thinnest high E string on top and the thickest low E string at the bottom. Numbers tell you which fret to press: 0 means play the string open and 3 means the third fret. Read left to right, and play numbers stacked on top of each other at the same time."],
  ["what do h and p mean in guitar tabs | guitar tab symbols", "Common tab symbols: **h** = hammer-on, **p** = pull-off, **/** = slide up, **\\** = slide down, **b** = bend the string, **x** = muted hit and **~** = vibrato. Many tabs don't show rhythm, so it helps to listen to the song too."],
  ["how to read a guitar chord chart | guitar chord diagram explained", "A **chord diagram** is a picture of the guitar neck standing upright: vertical lines are strings (low E on the left) and horizontal lines are frets. Dots show where to press, often with finger numbers, an **O** above a string means play it open and an **X** means don't play it."],
  ["what is a capo | what does a capo do", "A **capo** is a clamp that presses down all the strings at one fret. It raises the pitch of every string, so you can play in a higher key using the same easy chord shapes."],
  ["what is standard guitar tuning | how to tune a guitar", "**Standard tuning** from the thickest string to the thinnest is **E A D G B E**. A fun way to remember it is \"Eddie Ate Dynamite, Good Bye Eddie.\" The easiest way to tune is with a clip-on tuner or tuning app, turning each tuning peg slowly until the note matches."],
  ["what is a fret | what are frets on a guitar", "**Frets** are the thin metal strips across a guitar's neck. Pressing a string just behind a fret shortens the vibrating part of the string, and each fret raises the pitch by one half step."],
  ["what are voice types | soprano alto tenor bass | satb meaning", "Singing voices are grouped by range. **Soprano** is the highest and **alto** is lower, both usually sung by women and children. **Tenor** is a higher male voice and **bass** is the lowest. Choir music for these four parts is called **SATB**."],
  ["what is a mezzo soprano | what is a baritone voice", "A **mezzo-soprano** sings between soprano and alto. A **baritone** sings between tenor and bass and is the most common adult male voice."],
  ["what does a cappella mean | what is a cappella singing", "**A cappella** means singing with no instruments at all, just voices. The phrase is Italian for \"in the style of the chapel.\" Singers often imitate instrument sounds and beats with their voices."],
  ["what is a duet | what is a trio | what is a quartet", "A **duet** is music for two performers, a **trio** for three and a **quartet** for four. Larger groups are a **quintet** (5), **sextet** (6), **septet** (7) and **octet** (8)."],
  ["how does a piano work | is a piano a string or percussion instrument", "When you press a piano key, a felt-covered **hammer** strikes tightly stretched metal strings inside, and the vibrations are made louder by a big wooden soundboard. Because hammers hit strings, the piano is counted as both a string and a percussion instrument, though it's usually grouped with keyboards. It was invented by Bartolomeo Cristofori in Italy around 1700."],
  ["what do the pedals on a piano do | piano pedals explained", "Most pianos have up to three pedals. The right one is the **sustain (damper) pedal**, which lets notes keep ringing after you lift your fingers. The left is the **soft pedal**, and on grand pianos the middle one is the **sostenuto pedal**, which holds only the notes already pressed down."],
  ["who were famous pianists of the past | greatest pianists in history", "Legendary pianists include **Franz Liszt**, the 1800s superstar whose concerts caused \"Lisztomania,\" **Clara Schumann**, **Frédéric Chopin** and **Sergei Rachmaninoff**. In jazz, **Art Tatum**, **Thelonious Monk** and **Duke Ellington** were giants of the keyboard."],
  ["who was franz liszt | tell me about liszt", "**Franz Liszt** (1811–1886) was a Hungarian composer and perhaps the most dazzling pianist of the 1800s. Fans went so wild at his concerts that people called it \"Lisztomania.\" He wrote very difficult pieces like the Hungarian Rhapsodies and helped invent the modern solo piano recital."],
  ["how does a guitar make sound | acoustic vs electric guitar", "When you pluck or strum a guitar string it vibrates. On an **acoustic guitar**, the hollow wooden body makes that vibration loud enough to hear. An **electric guitar** usually has a solid body and needs an amplifier, because its sound is picked up electrically."],
  ["how does an electric guitar work | what are guitar pickups", "An electric guitar has **pickups**, small magnets wrapped in wire under the strings. When the steel strings vibrate, they create a tiny electric signal that travels through a cable to an **amplifier**, which makes it loud. Effects pedals can change the sound with distortion, echo and more."],
  ["what is a bass guitar | bass guitar vs guitar", "A **bass guitar** usually has four thick strings tuned E A D G, an octave lower than a guitar's lowest four strings. Bass players work closely with the drummer to lock in the groove and fill out the low end of the band's sound."],
  ["who were famous guitarists of the past | greatest guitarists in history", "Famous guitar legends include **Andrés Segovia**, who brought classical guitar to concert halls, **Django Reinhardt**, a jazz genius who played brilliantly with only two working fingers on his fretting hand, blues masters **B.B. King** and **Robert Johnson**, rock & roll pioneer **Chuck Berry** and the wildly inventive **Jimi Hendrix**."],
  ["how does a violin work | how do you play the violin", "A **violin** has four strings tuned G D A E. You draw a **bow** strung with horsehair across the strings to make them vibrate, and the hollow wooden body amplifies the sound. You change the pitch by pressing the strings against the fingerboard, which has no frets, so players must place their fingers by ear."],
  ["why do violinists put rosin on the bow | what is rosin", "**Rosin** is a hardened tree resin that string players rub on their bow hair. It makes the hair slightly sticky so it can grip the string and make it vibrate. Without rosin, a bow just slides silently across the strings!"],
  ["who were famous violinists | greatest violinists of the past", "**Niccolò Paganini** (1782–1840) was so astonishing that rumors said he had magical powers, and his 24 Caprices are still some of the hardest violin music. Other legends include **Fritz Kreisler**, **Jascha Heifetz** and **David Oistrakh**."],
  ["what is a stradivarius | why are stradivarius violins so expensive", "A **Stradivarius** is a violin, viola or cello made by **Antonio Stradivari** in Cremona, Italy, mostly in the late 1600s and early 1700s. Only about 650 of his instruments survive, and they're prized for their beautiful sound and history, so some have sold for millions of dollars."],
  ["what is a viola | viola vs violin", "A **viola** looks like a violin but is a bit bigger and plays lower, with strings tuned C G D A. It has a warmer, darker voice and reads music in the alto clef. In an orchestra, violas usually play the middle harmonies."],
  ["how does a cello work | what is a cello | famous cellists", "The **cello** is a large string instrument played sitting down, resting on a metal spike called an endpin. Its strings are tuned C G D A, an octave below the viola, and its rich voice is often compared to a human voice. **Pablo Casals** and **Jacqueline du Pré** were legendary cellists."],
  ["what is a double bass | what is an upright bass", "The **double bass** is the biggest and lowest instrument in the string family, usually taller than the person playing it! Its strings are tuned E A D G. It's played with a bow in orchestras and is often plucked in jazz, where it's called an upright bass."],
  ["how does a harp work | how many strings does a harp have", "A concert **harp** has 47 strings that you pluck with your fingers, and seven foot pedals that change the strings' pitch so it can play in any key. The C strings are usually colored red and the F strings black or blue, so harpists can find their place. Harps are one of the oldest instruments in the world."],
  ["what is a ukulele | where does the ukulele come from", "The **ukulele** is a small four-stringed instrument that became popular in **Hawaii** in the 1880s, based on a little guitar brought by Portuguese immigrants. Its strings are usually tuned G C E A. It's light, cheerful sounding and a great first instrument."],
  ["what is a banjo | how does a banjo work", "A **banjo** has a round body with a tight drum-like head that makes its bright, twangy sound. It developed from instruments brought to the Americas by enslaved Africans and became central to folk, country and bluegrass music."],
  ["what is a mandolin | how many strings does a mandolin have", "A **mandolin** is a small, pear-shaped string instrument from Italy with **eight strings in four pairs**, tuned like a violin (G D A E). It's played with a pick and makes a bright, sparkly sound, often with fast repeated notes called tremolo."],
  ["what is a sitar | how does a sitar work", "The **sitar** is a long-necked string instrument from India with a gourd body. Besides the strings that are plucked, it has extra **sympathetic strings** that ring along on their own, giving it its shimmering sound. **Ravi Shankar** made it famous around the world."],
  ["how does a flute work | is a flute a woodwind or brass", "You play a **flute** by blowing across a hole near one end, the way you blow over a bottle, which makes the air inside vibrate. Pressing keys changes the length of the air column and the pitch. Even though modern flutes are metal, they're **woodwinds**, because early ones were wooden and the sound isn't made by buzzing lips."],
  ["what is a piccolo | piccolo vs flute", "A **piccolo** is a half-size flute that plays an octave higher, making it the highest-pitched instrument in the orchestra. Its bright, piercing sound can be heard over a whole marching band."],
  ["how does a clarinet work | what is a clarinet", "A **clarinet** is a woodwind with a **single reed**, a thin strip of cane that vibrates against the mouthpiece when you blow. It has a huge range, from warm low notes to bright high ones. **Benny Goodman**, the \"King of Swing,\" made it a star of jazz."],
  ["what is an oboe | why does the oboe tune the orchestra", "An **oboe** is a woodwind played with a **double reed**, two thin pieces of cane that vibrate against each other. Its clear, steady tone carries well and doesn't drift easily, so the oboist plays an A for the whole orchestra to tune to before a concert."],
  ["what is a bassoon | how big is a bassoon", "The **bassoon** is the low voice of the woodwind family, played with a double reed. Its tube is nearly 3 meters (about 9 feet) long but is folded in half, so it's taller than most players when standing. It can sound serious, sweet or comical, like the grandfather in \"Peter and the Wolf.\""],
  ["how does a saxophone work | is a saxophone a woodwind or brass", "The **saxophone** is made of brass but counts as a **woodwind**, because it makes sound with a single reed like a clarinet. It was invented by Belgian instrument maker **Adolphe Sax** in the 1840s. Jazz legends **Charlie Parker** and **John Coltrane** made it famous."],
  ["what is a recorder instrument | how do you play a recorder", "The **recorder** is a simple woodwind with a whistle-like mouthpiece, so you just blow gently and cover the holes with your fingers. It's popular in schools but also has a long history, with lots of music written for it in the Renaissance and Baroque periods."],
  ["how do brass instruments work | why do you buzz your lips on a trumpet", "Brass players make sound by **buzzing their lips** into a cup-shaped mouthpiece. The buzz makes the air in the long metal tube vibrate. Players change notes by tightening or loosening their lips and by using valves or a slide to change the length of the tube."],
  ["how does a trumpet work | famous trumpet players", "A **trumpet** has about 1.5 meters (5 feet) of coiled tubing and **three valves**. Pressing the valves sends air through extra loops of tubing, lowering the pitch. Legendary trumpet players include **Louis Armstrong**, **Dizzy Gillespie** and **Miles Davis**."],
  ["how does a trombone work | what is a trombone slide", "Instead of valves, a **trombone** has a long **slide** that makes the tube longer or shorter. Pushing the slide out lowers the pitch, and there are seven main slide positions. Because the slide moves smoothly, trombones can glide between notes in a fun \"wah-wah\" way called a glissando."],
  ["what is a french horn | how does a french horn work", "The **French horn** has about 3.7 meters (12 feet) or more of tubing coiled into a circle, ending in a wide bell. Players keep one hand inside the bell to shape the sound and adjust pitch. Its warm, round tone is perfect for heroic movie themes."],
  ["what is a tuba | how big is a tuba", "The **tuba** is the biggest and lowest brass instrument. A large tuba can have around 5.5 meters (18 feet) of tubing wound up inside it. It gives bands and orchestras their deep, solid foundation."],
  ["what are the parts of a drum kit | drum set pieces", "A standard **drum kit** has a **bass (kick) drum** played with a foot pedal, a **snare drum** with a crisp rattle, **tom-toms** of different sizes, a **hi-hat** (two cymbals worked with a pedal) and **crash** and **ride cymbals**. The drummer keeps the beat for the whole band."],
  ["what makes a snare drum rattle | snare drum vs bass drum", "A **snare drum** has wires called **snares** stretched across its bottom head, which buzz against it to make a sharp, crackling sound. The **bass drum** is much bigger and makes a deep \"boom\" that you often feel as much as hear."],
  ["what are timpani | what is a kettledrum", "**Timpani** (kettledrums) are large copper bowls covered with a drumhead. Unlike most drums they play definite pitches, and a foot pedal changes how tight the head is so the timpanist can change notes. They add thunder and drama to orchestral music."],
  ["xylophone vs marimba vs glockenspiel | what is the difference between a xylophone and a glockenspiel", "All three are played by striking bars with mallets and are laid out like a piano keyboard. A **xylophone** has wooden bars and a bright, dry sound, a **marimba** has wooden bars with long resonator tubes for a deeper, warmer sound, and a **glockenspiel** has metal bars and a high, bell-like sound."],
  ["how are cymbals made | what is a triangle instrument", "**Cymbals** are thin, round plates made of bronze alloy, hammered and shaped to shimmer or crash when hit. The **triangle** is a steel bar bent into a triangle and struck with a metal beater, making a bright ringing sound. Both belong to the percussion family."],
  ["what are percussion instruments | examples of percussion instruments", "**Percussion instruments** make sound when you hit, shake or scrape them. Some have definite pitches, like timpani and xylophone, and others don't, like snare drum, tambourine, maracas, cymbals, triangle and cowbell. Percussion is probably the oldest instrument family of all."],
  ["who were famous drummers | greatest drummers of the past", "Famous drummers include jazz stars **Gene Krupa** and **Buddy Rich**, known for their lightning-fast solos, **Ringo Starr** of the Beatles and **John Bonham** of Led Zeppelin, famous for his huge, powerful sound."],
  ["how does a pipe organ work | what is the biggest instrument", "A **pipe organ** pushes air through rows of pipes, each tuned to one note, using keyboards for the hands and a pedal keyboard for the feet. Knobs called **stops** choose which sets of pipes sound. Big organs can have tens of thousands of pipes, making them the largest musical instruments in the world."],
  ["how does an accordion work | what is an accordion", "An **accordion** has folding **bellows** that you squeeze and pull to push air over small metal reeds that vibrate. One side has piano-style keys or buttons for the melody, and the other has buttons for bass notes and chords. It's popular in folk music from Europe to Latin America."],
  ["how does a harmonica work | how do you play a harmonica", "A **harmonica** has small metal reeds inside that vibrate when you breathe through its holes. Blowing out gives some notes and drawing in gives others. It's small enough to fit in a pocket and is a favorite in blues, folk and country music."],
  ["how do bagpipes work | where do bagpipes come from", "**Bagpipes** have a bag the player fills with air, then squeezes under the arm to push air steadily into the pipes. A pipe called the **chanter** plays the melody while **drones** hum constant notes underneath. They're famous in Scotland but are played in many countries, from Ireland to Spain to Turkey."],
  ["what is a synthesizer | how does a synthesizer work", "A **synthesizer** creates sounds electronically by generating and shaping electric signals called waveforms. It can imitate real instruments or make completely new sounds. Synthesizers like the Moog became popular in the 1960s and 70s and are now central to pop, film and electronic music."],
  ["what is a theremin | how do you play a theremin", "The **theremin** is an electronic instrument you play **without touching it**! Moving one hand near an antenna changes the pitch and moving the other hand near a second antenna changes the volume. Invented by Leon Theremin around 1920, its spooky, wavy sound was used in old science-fiction movies."],
  ["what is a harpsichord | harpsichord vs piano", "A **harpsichord** looks a bit like a piano, but its keys make small picks **pluck** the strings instead of hammers striking them. It was very popular in the Baroque era. Because it plucks, you can't make notes much louder or softer by pressing harder, which the later piano could do."],
  ["what is a didgeridoo | how do you play a didgeridoo", "The **didgeridoo** is a long wooden wind instrument created by Aboriginal peoples of northern Australia, traditionally made from a eucalyptus branch hollowed out by termites. Players buzz their lips to make a deep drone and often use **circular breathing**, breathing in through the nose while pushing air out with the cheeks, to play without stopping."],
  ["what is a steel drum | what is a steelpan", "The **steelpan** (steel drum) was invented in **Trinidad and Tobago** in the 1930s and 1940s, originally made from old oil drums. The top is hammered into sections that each play a different note. It has a bright, cheerful sound and is often called the only acoustic instrument invented in the 20th century."],
  ["what is a djembe | what is a tabla", "A **djembe** is a goblet-shaped drum from West Africa, played with bare hands to make deep bass tones and sharp slaps. The **tabla** is a pair of hand drums from India, played with fingertips and palms to create amazingly detailed rhythms."],
  ["what is a koto | what is an erhu | traditional asian instruments", "The **koto** is Japan's national instrument, a long zither with 13 strings played with finger picks. The **erhu** is a Chinese two-stringed fiddle played with a bow trapped between the strings, and it can sound like a singing voice. Other famous Asian instruments include the Chinese **pipa** and the Japanese **shamisen**."],
  ["how is an orchestra arranged | where do instruments sit in an orchestra", "In a typical orchestra, the **strings** sit at the front closest to the conductor, the **woodwinds** sit in the middle, the **brass** behind them and the **percussion** at the very back. Quieter instruments are in front so they aren't drowned out by the louder ones."],
  ["what is a concertmaster | what does first chair mean", "The **concertmaster** is the leader of the first violins and the most important player after the conductor. They lead the tuning and play violin solos. **First chair** means the top player in a section."],
  ["what is the highest instrument in the orchestra | what is the lowest instrument in the orchestra", "The **piccolo** usually plays the highest notes in the orchestra. The lowest are the **contrabassoon**, the **tuba** and the **double bass**, and a big pipe organ can go lower still."],
  ["is the voice an instrument | how does the human voice make sound", "Yes, many musicians call the voice the oldest instrument! Air from your lungs passes through your **vocal folds** in your throat, making them vibrate. Your throat, mouth and nose then shape that buzz into words and notes."],
  ["what are the periods of classical music | classical music eras in order", "Western classical music is often divided into eras: **Medieval** (about 500–1400), **Renaissance** (1400–1600), **Baroque** (1600–1750), **Classical** (1750–1820), **Romantic** (1820–1900) and the **20th century and modern** era. Each period has its own style, instruments and famous composers."],
  ["what is medieval music | what is gregorian chant", "Much surviving **medieval music** was sung in churches and monasteries. **Gregorian chant** is a single melody line sung in Latin with no harmony and no steady beat, giving a peaceful, floating sound. Wandering poet-musicians called **troubadours** also sang songs of love and adventure."],
  ["who was hildegard of bingen", "**Hildegard of Bingen** (1098–1179) was a German abbess, writer, scientist and composer. She's one of the earliest composers whose name and many works we know, and her soaring chants are still performed today."],
  ["what is renaissance music | renaissance composers", "**Renaissance music** (about 1400–1600) is known for smooth, flowing harmonies where several voice parts weave together, called **polyphony**. Famous composers include Palestrina, Josquin des Prez and Thomas Tallis. Instruments like the lute and recorder were popular."],
  ["what is baroque music | baroque era composers", "**Baroque music** (about 1600–1750) is full of energy, decoration and contrast, often with a steady bass line called the **basso continuo**. The harpsichord was a key instrument, and the era gave us the concerto, the fugue and opera. Top composers were Bach, Handel and Vivaldi."],
  ["what is handel's messiah", "**George Frideric Handel** (1685–1759) was a German-born Baroque composer who spent most of his career in London. His most famous work is **Messiah** (1741), with its \"Hallelujah\" chorus, during which audiences traditionally stand up. He also wrote \"Water Music\" and \"Music for the Royal Fireworks.\""],
  ["what is the classical period in music | classical era composers", "The **Classical period** (about 1750–1820) valued balance, clarity and elegant melodies. The symphony, the string quartet and the piano sonata became important forms. The biggest names are **Haydn**, **Mozart** and the young **Beethoven**."],
  ["who is the father of the symphony", "**Joseph Haydn** (1732–1809) was an Austrian composer often called the **\"Father of the Symphony\"** and the \"Father of the String Quartet.\" He wrote over 100 symphonies, including the \"Surprise\" Symphony, which has a sudden loud chord meant to wake up sleepy listeners! He was a friend of Mozart and a teacher of Beethoven."],
  ["what is the romantic period in music | romantic era composers", "The **Romantic period** (about 1820–1900) was all about big emotions, imagination, nature and storytelling. Orchestras got bigger and pieces got longer and more dramatic. Famous Romantic composers include Schubert, Chopin, Schumann, Liszt, Brahms, Verdi and Tchaikovsky."],
  ["tell me about franz schubert", "**Franz Schubert** (1797–1828) was an Austrian composer who wrote over 600 songs for voice and piano, plus symphonies and chamber music, even though he died at just 31. His famous works include the \"Unfinished\" Symphony, \"Ave Maria\" and the \"Trout\" Quintet."],
  ["what is brahms lullaby", "**Johannes Brahms** (1833–1897) was a German Romantic composer known for his rich symphonies, piano music and the Hungarian Dances. His gentle **\"Lullaby\"** (\"Wiegenlied\") is one of the most famous bedtime songs in the world."],
  ["famous verdi operas", "**Giuseppe Verdi** (1813–1901) was Italy's most beloved opera composer. His operas include **Aida**, **La traviata** and **Rigoletto**, which contains the catchy aria \"La donna è mobile.\" His music became a symbol of Italian national pride."],
  ["who was puccini | famous puccini operas", "**Giacomo Puccini** (1858–1924) was an Italian composer of some of the world's most popular operas, including **La bohème**, **Tosca**, **Madama Butterfly** and **Turandot**. Turandot contains the famous tenor aria \"Nessun dorma.\""],
  ["what is clair de lune", "**Claude Debussy** (1862–1918) was a French composer whose dreamy, shimmering music is often linked to Impressionist painting. His piano piece **\"Clair de lune\"** (\"Moonlight\") is one of the most loved pieces ever written. He also wrote \"La mer,\" a musical portrait of the sea."],
  ["what is the rite of spring", "**Igor Stravinsky** (1882–1971) was a Russian composer who wrote the ballets **The Firebird** and **The Rite of Spring**. The Rite's wild rhythms and clashing sounds were so shocking that its 1913 premiere in Paris caused an uproar in the audience. Today it's considered a masterpiece of modern music."],
  ["what is rhapsody in blue", "**George Gershwin** (1898–1937) was an American composer who blended jazz with classical music. His **Rhapsody in Blue** (1924) starts with a famous clarinet slide, and he also wrote the opera **Porgy and Bess** and many popular songs with his brother Ira."],
  ["who was dvorak | what is the new world symphony", "**Antonín Dvořák** (1841–1904) was a Czech composer who lived in America for a few years in the 1890s. There he wrote his Symphony No. 9, **\"From the New World,\"** inspired by the sounds and spirit of America. Its gentle slow movement is one of the most famous tunes in classical music."],
  ["who was saint-saens | what is the carnival of the animals", "**Camille Saint-Saëns** (1835–1921) was a French composer. His fun suite **The Carnival of the Animals** describes animals in music: lions roar on the piano, elephants lumber on the double bass, kangaroos hop and **\"The Swan\"** glides on a beautiful cello melody."],
  ["what is peter and the wolf | who wrote peter and the wolf", "**Peter and the Wolf** (1936) is a musical story by Russian composer **Sergei Prokofiev**, written to introduce children to the orchestra. Each character has its own instrument: the bird is the flute, the duck the oboe, the cat the clarinet, Grandfather the bassoon, the wolf three French horns and Peter the strings. A narrator tells the story as the music plays."],
  ["who was holst | what is the planets suite", "**Gustav Holst** (1874–1934) was an English composer best known for **The Planets**, a suite with one movement for each of seven planets, including the thundering \"Mars\" and the joyful \"Jupiter.\" Earth isn't included, and Pluto hadn't been discovered yet when he wrote it."],
  ["who wrote in the hall of the mountain king | who was edvard grieg", "**Edvard Grieg** (1843–1907) was a Norwegian composer. **\"In the Hall of the Mountain King\"** comes from his music for the play *Peer Gynt*, and it starts quietly and slowly, then gets faster and louder as trolls chase the hero. \"Morning Mood\" from the same work is also very famous."],
  ["who was mendelssohn | who wrote the wedding march", "**Felix Mendelssohn** (1809–1847) was a German composer and child prodigy who wrote music for Shakespeare's *A Midsummer Night's Dream* as a teenager. Its **\"Wedding March\"** is played at countless weddings. He also helped bring Bach's music back into fashion."],
  ["who was rachmaninoff | tell me about rachmaninoff", "**Sergei Rachmaninoff** (1873–1943) was a Russian composer and one of the greatest pianists ever. His huge hands could stretch across about 13 piano keys, and his Piano Concerto No. 2 and \"Rhapsody on a Theme of Paganini\" are full of sweeping, emotional melodies."],
  ["who was clara schumann", "**Clara Schumann** (1819–1896) was a German pianist and composer, and one of the most famous concert pianists of the 1800s. She toured Europe for decades, composed piano music and songs, and championed the music of her husband Robert Schumann and their friend Brahms."],
  ["what is a concerto | what is a sonata", "A **concerto** is a piece for a solo instrument, like a piano or violin, accompanied by an orchestra, usually in three movements. A **sonata** is a piece for one instrument, or one instrument with piano. Both often have fast, slow and fast movements."],
  ["what is a fugue | what is counterpoint", "**Counterpoint** is the art of combining two or more independent melodies so they sound good together. A **fugue** is a type of counterpoint where one short tune, called the subject, is introduced and then imitated by other voices one after another. Bach was the great master of the fugue."],
  ["what is chamber music | what is a string quartet", "**Chamber music** is classical music for a small group, with one player per part, originally meant to be played in a room or \"chamber\" rather than a big hall. The most famous kind is the **string quartet**: two violins, a viola and a cello."],
  ["what is an overture | what is a movement in music", "An **overture** is an instrumental opening piece played before an opera, ballet or musical. A **movement** is a self-contained section of a longer work like a symphony or concerto, and audiences traditionally wait until the end of all the movements to clap."],
  ["what is the history of opera | when was opera invented | famous operas", "**Opera** began in Italy around 1600, when musicians wanted to bring ancient Greek drama back to life with singing. Claudio Monteverdi's *L'Orfeo* (1607) is one of the earliest still performed. Famous operas include Mozart's *The Magic Flute*, Bizet's *Carmen*, Verdi's *Aida* and Puccini's *La bohème*."],
  ["what is an aria | what is a recitative", "An **aria** is a song for a solo voice in an opera, where a character expresses feelings and the singer shows off. A **recitative** is a more speech-like singing style used to move the story along between arias."],
  ["what is a musical | musical vs opera", "A **musical** tells a story with songs, spoken dialogue and dancing. Operas are usually sung all the way through in a classical style, while musicals mix talking and singing and often use pop, jazz or rock styles. Both are performed on stage with costumes and sets."],
  ["what are famous musicals | classic broadway musicals", "Classic stage musicals include **Show Boat**, **Oklahoma!**, **The Sound of Music**, **West Side Story**, **The Music Man**, **Annie**, **Les Misérables**, **The Phantom of the Opera** and **The Lion King**. Many have been made into movies too."],
  ["what is broadway | what is the west end", "**Broadway** is the theater district in New York City, with dozens of large theaters famous for musicals and plays. London's equivalent is the **West End**. Having a show \"on Broadway\" is one of the biggest goals in theater."],
  ["where did the blues come from | history of the blues", "The **blues** grew out of African American communities in the Deep South of the United States in the late 1800s. It drew on work songs, field hollers and spirituals, and it often expresses hardship with honesty and humor. The blues became the root of jazz, rock & roll, R&B and much more."],
  ["who were famous blues musicians | blues legends", "Famous blues musicians include **Bessie Smith**, the \"Empress of the Blues,\" **Robert Johnson**, **Lead Belly**, **Muddy Waters**, **Howlin' Wolf** and **B.B. King**, whose guitar he named \"Lucille.\""],
  ["who were famous jazz musicians | jazz legends", "Jazz legends include trumpeter **Louis Armstrong**, bandleader **Duke Ellington**, singers **Ella Fitzgerald** and **Billie Holiday**, saxophonists **Charlie Parker** and **John Coltrane**, and trumpeter **Miles Davis**."],
  ["who is the first lady of song", "**Ella Fitzgerald** (1917–1996), the \"First Lady of Song,\" was a jazz singer famous for her pure voice, perfect diction and incredible scat singing. Her \"Songbook\" albums of great American songs are classics, and she won 13 Grammy Awards."],
  ["what is ragtime", "**Ragtime** was a lively, syncopated piano style popular in America around 1895–1920 and an ancestor of jazz. **Scott Joplin**, the \"King of Ragtime,\" wrote \"Maple Leaf Rag\" and \"The Entertainer.\""],
  ["what is swing music | what is a big band", "**Swing** was the hugely popular dance music of the 1930s and 1940s, played by **big bands** of trumpets, trombones, saxophones and a rhythm section. It has a bouncy, danceable rhythm. Famous bandleaders included Benny Goodman, Count Basie, Duke Ellington and Glenn Miller."],
  ["what is bebop | what is bebop jazz", "**Bebop** was a fast, complex style of jazz that developed in the 1940s. Instead of dance tunes for big bands, small groups played rapid melodies and adventurous solos meant for listening. **Charlie Parker** and **Dizzy Gillespie** were its leaders."],
  ["what is scat singing | what does scat mean in jazz", "**Scat singing** is when a jazz singer improvises with nonsense syllables like \"doo-bee-doo-bah\" instead of words, using the voice like an instrument. Louis Armstrong helped make it popular in the 1920s, and Ella Fitzgerald became its greatest star."],
  ["what is gospel music | where did gospel music come from", "**Gospel music** is joyful, powerful Christian music that grew in African American churches in the early 1900s, building on spirituals and hymns. It features strong lead singers, choirs and call-and-response. Gospel deeply influenced soul, R&B and rock music."],
  ["how did rock and roll start | history of rock and roll", "**Rock & roll** exploded in the United States in the 1950s, mixing rhythm and blues, country, gospel and boogie-woogie. Its stars included **Chuck Berry**, **Little Richard**, **Fats Domino**, **Buddy Holly** and **Elvis Presley**. Its strong backbeat and electric guitars changed popular music forever."],
  ["why is elvis called the king of rock and roll", "**Elvis Presley** (1935–1977) was an American singer and actor whose energetic style and blend of blues, country and gospel made rock & roll a worldwide craze in the 1950s. Hits like \"Hound Dog\" and \"Jailhouse Rock\" earned him the nickname **\"the King of Rock and Roll.\"**"],
  ["who was chuck berry | father of rock and roll", "**Chuck Berry** (1926–2017) was an American guitarist, singer and songwriter often called one of the \"fathers of rock & roll.\" His guitar riffs and storytelling songs like \"Johnny B. Goode\" and \"Roll Over Beethoven\" inspired generations of rock musicians."],
  ["what was the british invasion", "**The Beatles** were a band from Liverpool, England: John Lennon, Paul McCartney, George Harrison and Ringo Starr. Starting in the early 1960s, they became the best-selling band in history with songs like \"Hey Jude\" and \"Here Comes the Sun.\" Their huge success in America started the **British Invasion** of UK bands."],
  ["what is punk rock | where did punk come from", "**Punk rock** appeared in the mid-1970s in New York and London. It used short, fast, loud songs with simple chords and a do-it-yourself attitude, as a reaction against long, fancy rock music. Early punk bands included the Ramones and the Sex Pistols."],
  ["what is heavy metal music | where did metal come from", "**Heavy metal** grew out of hard rock in the late 1960s and early 1970s in Britain and the US. It's known for loud, distorted guitars, powerful drums and dramatic vocals. Bands like Black Sabbath, Led Zeppelin and Deep Purple helped create the sound."],
  ["what is soul music | what was motown", "**Soul music** blends gospel, rhythm and blues and jazz into emotional, heartfelt songs. **Motown** was a record company founded in Detroit by Berry Gordy in 1959 that produced a string of hits by artists like the Supremes, Stevie Wonder, Marvin Gaye and the Jackson 5. Its catchy, polished style became known as \"the Motown sound.\""],
  ["what is funk music | what is disco", "**Funk**, from the late 1960s, focuses on a tight, danceable groove with punchy bass lines and syncopated rhythms; James Brown was its pioneer. **Disco** was a glittery dance music of the 1970s with a steady four-on-the-floor beat, strings and horns, played in dance clubs with mirror balls."],
  ["how did country music start | history of country music", "**Country music** began in the rural southern United States in the 1920s, from the folk songs of Appalachia, blues and church music. Early stars included the Carter Family and Jimmie Rodgers. **Nashville, Tennessee**, home of the radio show the Grand Ole Opry, became known as \"Music City.\""],
  ["what is bluegrass music | who invented bluegrass", "**Bluegrass** is a fast, acoustic style of American country music featuring banjo, fiddle, mandolin, guitar and upright bass, with high harmony singing. It was developed in the 1940s by **Bill Monroe** and his band, the Blue Grass Boys, named after Kentucky, the \"Bluegrass State.\""],
  ["what is folk music | what is a folk song", "**Folk music** is traditional music passed down by ordinary people, often by ear, from generation to generation. Folk songs tell stories about work, love, history and everyday life. Every culture has its own folk music, and many songs have no known author."],
  ["how did hip hop start | where did hip hop come from | history of rap", "**Hip-hop** began in the **Bronx, New York**, in the 1970s. A famous starting point is a 1973 party where DJ **Kool Herc** played the drum breaks of funk records over and over so people could dance longer. MCs began rhyming over the beats, and in 1979 \"Rapper's Delight\" by the Sugarhill Gang became the first big rap hit."],
  ["what are the four elements of hip hop | what is hip hop culture", "Hip-hop is a whole culture, traditionally described with four elements: **DJing** (mixing and scratching records), **MCing** (rapping), **breaking** (breakdancing) and **graffiti art**. Many people add a fifth element: knowledge."],
  ["what is rap | what is beatboxing", "**Rap** is rhythmic, rhyming speech performed over a beat, and it's a central part of hip-hop music. **Beatboxing** is making drum beats, bass lines and sound effects with just your mouth, lips and voice, often used to accompany rappers."],
  ["where did reggae come from", "**Reggae** developed in **Jamaica** in the late 1960s, growing from earlier styles called ska and rocksteady. It has a relaxed beat with guitar chops on the off-beats and a deep bass. **Bob Marley** (1945–1981) and the Wailers made reggae famous worldwide with songs like \"One Love\" and \"Three Little Birds.\""],
  ["what is ska music | what is calypso music", "**Ska** started in Jamaica in the late 1950s, with upbeat horns and guitar strums on the off-beats, and later led to reggae. **Calypso** comes from Trinidad and Tobago and features witty, rhythmic songs about everyday life and news, especially at Carnival."],
  ["what is salsa music | what is latin music", "**Salsa** is energetic dance music that took shape in New York City in the 1960s and 1970s, built on Cuban and Puerto Rican rhythms with lots of percussion, piano and horns. \"Latin music\" is a broad term covering many styles from Latin America and Spain, including salsa, merengue, cumbia, bachata, tango and reggaeton."],
  ["what is samba | what is bossa nova", "**Samba** is a lively Brazilian music and dance style with roots in African rhythms, and it's the heartbeat of the famous Carnival in Rio de Janeiro. **Bossa nova**, \"new trend,\" is a cool, gentle blend of samba and jazz from the late 1950s, made famous by \"The Girl from Ipanema.\""],
  ["what is flamenco | where does flamenco come from", "**Flamenco** is a passionate art form from **Andalusia** in southern Spain, closely tied to its Romani (Gitano) communities. It combines singing (cante), guitar playing (toque), dancing (baile) and rhythmic hand clapping. Dancers often stamp out complex rhythms with their heels."],
  ["what is mariachi | what instruments are in a mariachi band", "**Mariachi** is a traditional music style from Mexico, especially the state of Jalisco. A mariachi band typically has violins, trumpets, a Spanish guitar, a small high-pitched guitar called the **vihuela** and a large bass guitar called the **guitarrón**. Musicians often wear fancy charro suits and sombreros."],
  ["what is a raga | what is indian classical music", "**Indian classical music** is built on the **raga**, a melodic framework with its own set of notes, patterns and mood, often linked to a time of day or season. Rhythm is organized in cycles called **tala**. Musicians improvise within these rules, so performances can last a very long time."],
  ["what is gamelan | what is gamelan music", "A **gamelan** is a traditional ensemble from Indonesia, especially Java and Bali, made up mostly of bronze gongs, metal xylophone-like instruments and drums. The players interlock their parts to create shimmering, layered music for ceremonies, dance and shadow-puppet plays."],
  ["how did electronic music start | history of electronic music", "**Electronic music** uses electronic instruments and technology to create sound. Early pioneers experimented with tape recorders and inventions like the theremin, and synthesizers became widespread in the 1960s and 70s. The German group Kraftwerk inspired later styles like **house**, which began in Chicago, and **techno**, which began in Detroit, both in the 1980s."],
  ["what is house music | what is techno music", "**House music** began in Chicago clubs in the early 1980s, with a steady four-on-the-floor beat, soulful vocals and synthesizers. **Techno** developed in Detroit around the same time and has a more mechanical, futuristic sound. Both spread worldwide and inspired many other dance styles."],
  ["what does a dj do | what is sampling in music", "A **DJ** (disc jockey) plays and mixes recorded music, blending songs smoothly or creating new sounds by scratching and looping. **Sampling** means taking a short piece of an existing recording, like a drum beat or a horn line, and reusing it in a new song, which is a key technique in hip-hop and electronic music."],
  ["what is k-pop | what is k-pop as a genre | where did k-pop come from", "**K-pop** is popular music from **South Korea**. It mixes pop, hip-hop, R&B, electronic and rock with polished music videos, fashion and tightly synchronized dance routines. Its modern style is often traced to the early 1990s, and performers usually train for years in agencies before debuting in groups. Its global spread is part of the \"Korean Wave\" (Hallyu)."],
  ["what is j-pop | what is japanese pop music", "**J-pop** is the popular music of Japan, mixing Western pop and rock with Japanese styles. It's closely connected to anime, TV dramas and video games, and many anime theme songs are J-pop. The term became common in the 1990s."],
  ["what is a sea shanty | why did sailors sing shanties", "A **sea shanty** is a work song once sung by sailors on big sailing ships. A leader sang lines and the crew answered, which helped everyone pull ropes or turn the capstan together in rhythm. Famous shanties include \"Drunken Sailor\" and \"Leave Her, Johnny.\""],
  ["what is a barbershop quartet", "A **barbershop quartet** is a group of four singers performing a cappella in close, ringing harmonies. The **lead** sings the melody, the **tenor** sings above it and the **baritone** and **bass** sing below. The style became popular in America around 1900."],
  ["what is a lullaby | why do lullabies help babies sleep", "A **lullaby** is a soft, gentle song sung to help a baby fall asleep. Lullabies usually have slow, rocking rhythms and simple repeating melodies, which are soothing. Nearly every culture in the world has its own lullabies."],
  ["how was music recorded in the past | what is a phonograph | when was music first recorded", "In 1877 **Thomas Edison** invented the **phonograph**, which recorded sound as grooves on a cylinder wrapped in tinfoil. Soon flat discs called records took over, played on gramophones. Later came magnetic tape, cassettes, CDs and finally digital files and streaming."],
  ["what is a vinyl record | how does a record player work", "A **vinyl record** has one long spiral groove with tiny wiggles that match the shape of the sound waves. As the record spins, a needle (stylus) rides in the groove and vibrates, and those vibrations are turned into electric signals and amplified into music."],
  ["what was the renaissance in art | renaissance art | what is renaissance art", "The **Renaissance** (\"rebirth\") was a burst of art and learning that began in Italy in the 1300s–1400s and spread across Europe. Artists revived ideas from ancient Greece and Rome and studied anatomy, light and **perspective** to make paintings look real. Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo, Raphael and Botticelli were its stars."],
  ["what is baroque art | baroque art style", "**Baroque art** (about 1600–1750) is dramatic, emotional and full of movement, with strong contrasts of light and dark. Painters like **Caravaggio**, **Rembrandt** and **Rubens** and the sculptor **Bernini** wanted art to amaze and stir the viewer's feelings."],
  ["what is rococo art", "**Rococo** was a light, playful and elegant style from 1700s France that followed the Baroque. It featured pastel colors, curving shapes, gold decoration and scenes of carefree parties in gardens. Fragonard's painting \"The Swing\" is a famous example."],
  ["what is romanticism in art | romantic art", "**Romanticism** (late 1700s to mid-1800s) celebrated emotion, imagination and the awesome power of nature. Painters like **J.M.W. Turner**, **Caspar David Friedrich** and **Eugène Delacroix** painted stormy seas, misty mountains and dramatic events."],
  ["what is realism in art | realism art movement", "**Realism** was an art movement in mid-1800s France that showed ordinary people and everyday life honestly, rather than gods, heroes or idealized scenes. Gustave Courbet and Jean-François Millet, who painted farm workers, were leading Realists."],
  ["who were the impressionists | famous impressionist painters", "The **Impressionists** were a group of painters in 1870s France who painted outdoors with quick, visible brushstrokes to capture light and fleeting moments. Besides Claude Monet, the group included **Pierre-Auguste Renoir**, **Edgar Degas**, **Camille Pissarro**, **Berthe Morisot**, **Alfred Sisley** and **Mary Cassatt**."],
  ["what is post impressionism | post-impressionism artists", "**Post-Impressionism** (about 1886–1905) is the name for artists who built on Impressionism but went in their own directions, using bolder colors, stronger shapes and more personal expression. Its leading figures were **Vincent van Gogh**, **Paul Cézanne**, **Paul Gauguin** and **Georges Seurat**."],
  ["what is pointillism | who was georges seurat", "**Pointillism** is a painting technique using thousands of tiny dots of pure color that blend together in your eye when seen from a distance. It was developed by **Georges Seurat**, whose huge painting *A Sunday Afternoon on the Island of La Grande Jatte* (1884–1886) took him about two years to finish."],
  ["cubism art movement", "**Cubism** was started around 1907–1908 by **Pablo Picasso** and **Georges Braque**. Instead of showing an object from one viewpoint, Cubists broke it into geometric shapes and showed several sides at once. It was one of the most revolutionary ideas in modern art."],
  ["what is surrealism | surrealism art", "**Surrealism** began in the 1920s and explored dreams, the imagination and the unconscious mind. Surrealist artists like **Salvador Dalí**, **René Magritte** and **Joan Miró** painted strange, dreamlike scenes, such as melting clocks or a man whose face is hidden by an apple."],
  ["what is fauvism | who were the fauves", "**Fauvism** was a short-lived movement in early 1900s France known for wild, bright, unnatural colors, like a face painted green. Critics called the artists *les Fauves*, \"the wild beasts.\" **Henri Matisse** was its leader."],
  ["what is expressionism in art", "**Expressionism** was an early-1900s movement, especially in Germany, where artists distorted shapes and colors to express strong feelings rather than show how things look. Edvard Munch's *The Scream* was an important forerunner, and Wassily Kandinsky and Franz Marc were key members."],
  ["what is dada art | what is dadaism", "**Dada** was a rebellious art movement that began around 1916 in Zurich, during World War I. Dada artists made deliberately silly or shocking works to question what art is. Marcel Duchamp famously displayed everyday objects, like a bicycle wheel mounted on a stool, as art and called them \"readymades.\""],
  ["what is pop art | pop art movement", "**Pop Art** began in the 1950s in Britain and the US and used images from popular culture, such as comic strips, advertisements, celebrities and product packaging. Its bright colors and bold graphics made everyday things into art. **Andy Warhol** and **Roy Lichtenstein** are its best-known artists."],
  ["what is abstract expressionism", "**Abstract Expressionism** was an American movement of the 1940s and 1950s, centered in New York. Some artists, like **Jackson Pollock**, used energetic \"action painting,\" dripping and flinging paint, while others, like **Mark Rothko**, painted large fields of glowing color meant to create deep feelings."],
  ["what is art nouveau", "**Art Nouveau** (\"new art\") was a decorative style popular around 1890–1910. It used flowing, curving lines inspired by plants, flowers and long hair, and appeared in posters, jewelry, furniture and buildings. **Alphonse Mucha**'s posters and the Paris Métro entrances are classic examples."],
  ["what is art deco", "**Art Deco** was a stylish design movement of the 1920s and 1930s, featuring bold geometric shapes, zigzags, sunbursts and shiny materials. You can see it in buildings like New York's Chrysler Building, as well as in jewelry, posters and furniture of the era."],
  ["what is ukiyo-e | ukiyo-e japanese prints", "**Ukiyo-e**, \"pictures of the floating world,\" is a Japanese art form of woodblock prints and paintings that flourished from the 1600s to the 1800s. The prints showed actors, beautiful landscapes, legends and daily life. Artists like **Hokusai** and **Hiroshige** later inspired the European Impressionists."],
  ["what is minimalism in art | minimalist art", "**Minimalism** was an art movement of the 1960s that stripped art down to its simplest forms, such as plain geometric shapes, single colors and repeated units. The idea was that the artwork is just what you see, without hidden stories. Donald Judd and Agnes Martin were leading Minimalists."],
  ["what is street art | is graffiti art", "**Street art** is art made in public spaces, such as murals, stencils, stickers and graffiti. Modern graffiti grew in New York City in the 1970s alongside hip-hop. Painting on walls without permission is illegal, but many cities now invite artists to create amazing legal murals."],
  ["what is folk art | what is outsider art", "**Folk art** is art made by ordinary people, often without formal training, following traditions of their community, like quilts, carved toys, painted pottery and embroidery. **Outsider art** is made by self-taught artists working outside the art world entirely."],
  ["what is the great wave off kanagawa", "***The Great Wave off Kanagawa*** is a Japanese woodblock print made around 1831 by **Katsushika Hokusai**. It shows a giant claw-like wave about to crash over fishing boats, with Mount Fuji small in the background. Hokusai kept working into his late 80s and once said he hoped to finally become a true artist at 110!"],
  ["who was hiroshige | tell me about utagawa hiroshige", "**Utagawa Hiroshige** (1797–1858) was a Japanese ukiyo-e artist famous for poetic landscape prints, especially the series *The Fifty-three Stations of the Tōkaidō*, which shows stops along the road between Edo (Tokyo) and Kyoto. Van Gogh admired him so much that he copied some of his prints in oil paint."],
  ["what is the persistence of memory | who painted the melting clocks", "***The Persistence of Memory*** (1931) is a famous Surrealist painting by **Salvador Dalí** showing soft, melting pocket watches draped over a branch and a strange creature in a dreamy landscape. It's surprisingly small, only about 24 × 33 cm (roughly the size of a sheet of paper)."],
  ["tell me about dali", "**Salvador Dalí** (1904–1989) was a Spanish Surrealist painter known for strange, dreamlike pictures and his amazing upturned mustache. Besides painting, he made sculptures, jewelry, films and even worked on a Disney animation. His most famous painting is *The Persistence of Memory*."],
  ["what is the son of man painting", "**René Magritte** (1898–1967) was a Belgian Surrealist who painted ordinary things in puzzling ways. *The Son of Man* shows a man in a bowler hat with a green apple floating in front of his face, and *The Treachery of Images* shows a pipe with the words \"This is not a pipe,\" because it's only a picture of one!"],
  ["tell me about the last supper painting | who painted the last supper", "***The Last Supper*** is a huge wall painting by **Leonardo da Vinci**, made in the 1490s in a monastery dining hall in Milan, Italy. It shows Jesus and his twelve apostles at the moment he says one of them will betray him, capturing each person's reaction. Leonardo used an experimental technique, so the painting began flaking soon after he finished it, and it has been carefully restored."],
  ["what is the sistine chapel ceiling", "**Michelangelo** painted the ceiling of the **Sistine Chapel** in Vatican City between 1508 and 1512, covering it with more than 300 figures from Bible stories. The most famous scene is ***The Creation of Adam***, where God's and Adam's fingers almost touch. He painted standing on scaffolding, looking up for years."],
  ["what is the birth of venus", "***The Birth of Venus*** is a painting from the 1480s by the Italian Renaissance artist **Sandro Botticelli**. It shows the goddess Venus arriving at the shore standing on a giant scallop shell, blown by wind gods. It hangs in the Uffizi Gallery in Florence."],
  ["what is the school of athens", "**Raphael** (1483–1520) was one of the three great masters of the High Renaissance, along with Leonardo and Michelangelo, known for graceful, balanced paintings. His fresco ***The School of Athens*** in the Vatican shows ancient Greek thinkers like Plato and Aristotle gathered in a grand hall, painted with amazing perspective."],
  ["why are the ninja turtles named after artists | which renaissance artists are the ninja turtles named after", "The Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles are named after four Italian Renaissance artists: **Leonardo** da Vinci, **Michelangelo**, **Raphael** and **Donatello**, a sculptor famous for his bronze statue of David. The creators chose the names as a fun joke."],
  ["who was donatello the artist | who was donatello the sculptor", "**Donatello** (about 1386–1466) was an Italian sculptor from Florence and one of the founders of Renaissance art. His bronze **David** was one of the first free-standing nude statues since ancient times. He was very skilled at showing real emotions in stone and bronze."],
  ["what is the night watch", "**Rembrandt van Rijn** (1606–1669) was a Dutch master famous for his use of light and shadow and his honest self-portraits, of which he made dozens. His huge painting ***The Night Watch*** (1642) shows a city militia bursting into action. It's actually set in daytime, but old varnish made it look dark."],
  ["who was caravaggio | caravaggio paintings", "**Caravaggio** (1571–1610) was an Italian Baroque painter who used dramatic spotlight effects, with figures lit brightly against deep darkness. He painted Bible scenes using everyday people as models, making them look very real. His style influenced painters all over Europe."],
  ["what is las meninas | who was diego velazquez", "***Las Meninas*** (1656) is a famous painting by the Spanish court painter **Diego Velázquez**. It shows young Princess Margarita with her maids of honor, while Velázquez himself appears painting at a big canvas, and the king and queen are seen reflected in a mirror. It's a clever puzzle about who is watching whom."],
  ["who was artemisia gentileschi", "**Artemisia Gentileschi** (1593–about 1656) was an Italian Baroque painter and one of the most successful women artists of her time. She painted powerful, dramatic scenes, often of strong heroines from history and the Bible, and she was the first woman accepted into Florence's Academy of Art and Design."],
  ["what is the arnolfini portrait", "**Jan van Eyck** (about 1390–1441) was a Flemish painter famous for incredibly detailed oil paintings. In ***The Arnolfini Portrait*** (1434), a couple stands in a room, and a tiny convex mirror on the back wall reflects the whole scene, including two more figures. He helped make oil paint popular."],
  ["durer rhinoceros", "**Albrecht Dürer** (1471–1528) was a German Renaissance artist famous for detailed prints, drawings and watercolors. His woodcut of a **rhinoceros** (1515) was made from a written description and a sketch without ever seeing the animal, so it has armor-like plates, yet it was copied for centuries. His watercolor *Young Hare* is amazingly lifelike."],
  ["who was pieter bruegel | what is hunters in the snow", "**Pieter Bruegel the Elder** (about 1525–1569) was a Flemish painter known for lively scenes of peasant life, festivals and landscapes packed with tiny details. ***Hunters in the Snow*** (1565) shows hunters returning to a village while people skate on frozen ponds below."],
  ["who was jmw turner | who was john constable", "**J.M.W. Turner** (1775–1851) was an English painter of swirling, light-filled seascapes and storms that almost dissolve into color. **John Constable** (1776–1837), another English painter, loved peaceful countryside scenes, and his painting *The Hay Wain* shows a horse-drawn cart crossing a stream."],
  ["why did degas paint ballerinas", "**Edgar Degas** (1834–1917) was a French artist linked with the Impressionists, famous for paintings, pastels and sculptures of **ballet dancers**. He loved capturing movement and behind-the-scenes moments at rehearsals. His sculpture *Little Dancer Aged Fourteen* even wears a real fabric tutu."],
  ["tell me about pierre-auguste renoir", "**Pierre-Auguste Renoir** (1841–1919) was a French Impressionist painter known for joyful scenes of people enjoying life, glowing with soft light and color. His painting *Luncheon of the Boating Party* shows friends relaxing on a sunny balcony. He kept painting into old age, even with a brush tied to his hand."],
  ["who was mary cassatt", "**Mary Cassatt** (1844–1926) was an American painter who lived in Paris and exhibited with the Impressionists. She is best known for tender paintings of mothers and children in everyday moments, like bath time or reading together."],
  ["father of modern art", "**Paul Cézanne** (1839–1906) was a French Post-Impressionist who painted apples, mountains and people as solid shapes built from patches of color. He often painted Mont Sainte-Victoire near his home. Picasso called him \"the father of us all,\" and he is often called the father of modern art."],
  ["what are matisse cut-outs", "**Henri Matisse** (1869–1954) was a French artist famous for bright, joyful color and flowing lines. Late in life, when he couldn't stand to paint easily, he created colorful **paper cut-outs**, cutting shapes from painted paper and arranging them into pictures. He called it \"painting with scissors.\""],
  ["what is the kiss painting", "**Gustav Klimt** (1862–1918) was an Austrian painter known for decorative art covered in patterns and real **gold leaf**. His most famous painting, ***The Kiss*** (1907–1908), shows a couple wrapped in golden robes in a field of flowers. It hangs in the Belvedere museum in Vienna."],
  ["who was alphonse mucha", "**Alphonse Mucha** (1860–1939) was a Czech artist whose posters made Art Nouveau famous. His designs show graceful women with flowing hair surrounded by flowers, swirls and halos. Late in life he painted *The Slav Epic*, a series of 20 enormous paintings about Slavic history."],
  ["who was wassily kandinsky | who invented abstract art", "**Wassily Kandinsky** (1866–1944) was a Russian painter and one of the first to make fully **abstract** paintings, using colors and shapes with no recognizable objects. He believed colors could feel like music, and he named works \"Compositions\" and \"Improvisations,\" like musical pieces."],
  ["who was piet mondrian | mondrian paintings", "**Piet Mondrian** (1872–1944) was a Dutch painter famous for grids of black lines with rectangles of red, yellow and blue on white. He believed simple lines and primary colors could express harmony. His style has inspired countless designs, from clothing to buildings."],
  ["who was mark rothko", "**Mark Rothko** (1903–1970) was an American Abstract Expressionist known for huge canvases filled with soft-edged rectangles of glowing color stacked on top of each other. He hoped viewers would stand close and feel deep emotions in front of them."],
  ["what is drip painting", "**Jackson Pollock** (1912–1956) was an American Abstract Expressionist famous for **drip painting**. He laid huge canvases on the floor and dripped, poured and flicked paint onto them while moving around, creating tangled webs of color. People nicknamed him \"Jack the Dripper.\""],
  ["what are the campbell's soup cans", "**Andy Warhol** (1928–1987) was the leading American Pop artist. His *Campbell's Soup Cans* (1962) is a set of 32 paintings, one for each soup flavor at the time. He used silkscreen printing to repeat images of celebrities and products, making art look like mass production."],
  ["who was roy lichtenstein", "**Roy Lichtenstein** (1923–1997) was an American Pop artist who made huge paintings in the style of comic book panels, complete with speech bubbles and the tiny dots used in printing, called Ben-Day dots. His work *Whaam!* shows a fighter plane in a dramatic comic-style explosion."],
  ["who was keith haring | who was basquiat", "**Keith Haring** (1958–1990) was an American artist known for bold, cartoon-like figures, dancing people, barking dogs and glowing babies, often drawn in chalk in New York subway stations. **Jean-Michel Basquiat** (1960–1988) began as a street artist and became famous for energetic paintings mixing words, crowns, skulls and symbols."],
  ["georgia okeeffe flowers", "**Georgia O'Keeffe** (1887–1986) was an American painter called the \"Mother of American Modernism.\" She painted giant close-up flowers that fill the whole canvas, as well as skyscrapers, bones and the dramatic desert landscapes of New Mexico, where she lived for many years."],
  ["what is a mural", "A **mural** is a large painting made directly on a wall or ceiling. **Diego Rivera** (1886–1957) was a Mexican painter famous for huge murals showing Mexico's history, workers and everyday people, painted on public buildings so everyone could see them. He was married to artist Frida Kahlo."],
  ["what is american gothic | who painted american gothic", "***American Gothic*** (1930) by **Grant Wood** shows a serious-looking farmer holding a pitchfork beside a woman, standing in front of a house with a pointed Gothic-style window. The models were the artist's sister and his dentist! It's one of the most copied and parodied paintings in America."],
  ["what is nighthawks | who was edward hopper", "**Edward Hopper** (1882–1967) was an American painter known for quiet, lonely scenes of city life. ***Nighthawks*** (1942) shows a few people in a brightly lit corner diner late at night, seen through a big glass window from the dark, empty street."],
  ["what is guernica painting | why did picasso paint guernica", "***Guernica*** (1937) is a huge black, white and gray painting by **Pablo Picasso**. He made it in response to the bombing of the town of Guernica during the Spanish Civil War. Its twisted figures express the pain of war, and it became one of the most famous anti-war artworks ever."],
  ["what is the thinker statue", "***The Thinker*** is a bronze sculpture by French artist **Auguste Rodin** (1840–1917), showing a man sitting deep in thought with his chin on his hand. It was first designed around 1880 as part of a huge door called *The Gates of Hell* and was later made as its own statue. Rodin is often called the father of modern sculpture."],
  ["tell me about michelangelo's david | how tall is the statue of david", "Michelangelo carved his marble statue of **David** between 1501 and 1504, when he was in his twenties. It stands about 5.17 meters (17 feet) tall and shows the young hero calmly preparing to face Goliath. It's in the Accademia Gallery in Florence, Italy."],
  ["what is the venus de milo | why does the venus de milo have no arms", "The **Venus de Milo** is an ancient Greek marble statue, probably made around 150–125 BC and thought to show Aphrodite, goddess of love. It was found on the Greek island of Milos in 1820, already missing its arms, and nobody knows for sure how they were posed. It's displayed in the Louvre in Paris."],
  ["who made the statue of liberty | is the statue of liberty a sculpture", "Yes! The **Statue of Liberty** is a giant copper sculpture designed by French sculptor **Frédéric-Auguste Bartholdi**, with an inner iron framework engineered by Gustave Eiffel. France gave it to the United States, and it was dedicated in 1886. It was shiny copper-brown at first and slowly turned green as the copper weathered."],
  ["terracotta warriors", "The **Terracotta Army** is a collection of thousands of life-size clay soldiers, horses and chariots buried with China's first emperor, Qin Shi Huang, over 2,200 years ago. Farmers digging a well discovered them in 1974. Each soldier's face is a little bit different."],
  ["what are the lascaux cave paintings", "**Cave paintings** are some of humanity's oldest art. The caves of **Lascaux** in France contain hundreds of paintings of horses, deer and bulls made about 17,000 years ago with natural pigments. Even older cave art, more than 40,000 years old, has been found in places like Indonesia."],
  ["what is perspective in art | what is a vanishing point | one point perspective", "**Perspective** is a way of drawing that makes a flat picture look three-dimensional. In **one-point perspective**, lines going away from you, like railroad tracks or a hallway, all meet at a single **vanishing point** on the horizon line. Renaissance artists perfected this technique."],
  ["what is two point perspective | how to draw in two point perspective", "In **two-point perspective**, there are two vanishing points on the horizon line, usually far apart. It's great for drawing a building or box seen from its corner, because each side's lines run toward a different vanishing point while vertical lines stay straight up and down."],
  ["what is foreshortening in art", "**Foreshortening** is drawing something that points toward the viewer so it looks shorter and closer, like an arm reaching out at you or the soles of someone's feet when they're lying down facing you. It's a tricky but powerful way to show depth."],
  ["how do i shade a drawing | what is shading in art | how to shade with a pencil", "**Shading** shows where light hits an object and where shadows fall, making it look 3D. First decide where the light comes from, then make the side away from the light darker. Use light pressure and build up layers slowly, and blend with a tissue or blending stump for smooth tones."],
  ["what is hatching | what is cross hatching | what is stippling", "**Hatching** is shading with lots of parallel lines, and drawing them closer together makes an area darker. **Cross-hatching** adds layers of lines crossing in different directions for even darker tones. **Stippling** creates shading with many tiny dots."],
  ["what is chiaroscuro | what is light and shadow in art", "**Chiaroscuro** (kee-ar-oh-SKOO-roh), Italian for \"light-dark,\" is the use of strong contrasts between light and shadow to make figures look three-dimensional and dramatic. Leonardo, Caravaggio and Rembrandt were masters of it."],
  ["what is a tint and a shade | what is value in art", "**Value** means how light or dark a color is. Adding white to a color makes a lighter **tint** (like pink from red), adding black makes a darker **shade** and adding gray makes a **tone**. A value scale shows steps from white to black."],
  ["what are the elements of art | 7 elements of art", "The seven **elements of art** are the basic building blocks artists use: **line**, **shape**, **form** (3D shape), **color**, **value** (light and dark), **texture** and **space**."],
  ["what are the principles of design | principles of art", "The **principles of design** describe how artists arrange the elements of art. They usually include **balance**, **contrast**, **emphasis**, **movement**, **pattern**, **rhythm**, **proportion** and **unity**."],
  ["what is the rule of thirds | what is composition in art", "**Composition** is how the parts of an artwork are arranged. The **rule of thirds** is a handy guide: imagine the picture divided by two horizontal and two vertical lines into nine boxes, then place important things along those lines or where they cross. It usually looks more interesting than putting everything dead center."],
  ["what is negative space in art", "**Negative space** is the empty area around and between the main subjects of an artwork. Paying attention to it helps you draw shapes accurately, and clever designs use it to hide second images, like the arrow hidden between two letters in some logos."],
  ["what is contour drawing | what is blind contour drawing", "**Contour drawing** focuses on the outlines and edges of an object using a continuous line. In **blind contour drawing**, you look only at the object, never at your paper, while you draw slowly. The results look funny, but it trains your eye and hand to work together."],
  ["what is a still life | what is a landscape painting | what is a portrait", "A **still life** shows objects that don't move, like fruit, flowers or vases. A **landscape** shows outdoor scenery such as mountains, fields or the sea. A **portrait** shows a person, often focusing on their face, and a **self-portrait** is when an artist portrays themselves."],
  ["how do you paint with watercolors | watercolor techniques for beginners", "**Watercolor** is transparent, so you usually work from light to dark and let the white paper show through for highlights. Try **wet-on-wet** (painting onto wet paper for soft blurry blends) and **wet-on-dry** (painting on dry paper for crisp edges). Use thick watercolor paper so it doesn't wrinkle."],
  ["what is oil painting | how long does oil paint take to dry", "**Oil paint** is pigment mixed with oil, usually linseed oil. It dries very slowly, taking days or weeks to feel dry and months to dry completely, which lets artists blend colors smoothly and make changes. It became popular in Europe in the 1400s, and most famous old master paintings are in oil."],
  ["how to paint with acrylics | acrylic painting tips", "**Acrylic paint** is water-based but dries quickly into a tough, waterproof layer, often within minutes. Keep your brushes wet and wash them before the paint dries, and use a spray bottle to keep paint on your palette moist. Acrylics work on canvas, paper, wood and many other surfaces."],
  ["what is gouache | what is tempera paint", "**Gouache** (gwash) is like watercolor but opaque, so it covers what's underneath and dries to a flat, matte finish, which illustrators love. **Egg tempera** is pigment mixed with egg yolk, used for many medieval and early Renaissance paintings before oil paint took over."],
  ["what is a fresco | how is a fresco painted", "A **fresco** is a painting made on wet plaster. As the plaster dries, the paint becomes part of the wall, which makes it last for centuries. Artists had to work quickly, painting only as much fresh plaster as they could finish in a day. The Sistine Chapel ceiling is a fresco."],
  ["what is impasto | what is a palette knife", "**Impasto** is applying paint so thickly that brushstrokes or knife marks stand up from the surface, adding texture. A **palette knife** is a flat, flexible blade used to mix paint or to spread it thickly on the canvas. Van Gogh often used impasto."],
  ["what is an easel | what is a palette in art | what is a canvas", "An **easel** is a stand that holds a painting upright while you work. A **palette** is the board or tray where artists mix paint, and the word also means the range of colors an artist uses. A **canvas** is strong cloth, usually cotton or linen, stretched over a wooden frame to paint on."],
  ["what is collage | what is mixed media art", "A **collage** is artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, photos, fabric and other materials onto a surface. **Mixed media** means combining different materials in one artwork, like paint, ink, collage and found objects. Picasso and Braque helped make collage a serious art form."],
  ["what is a mosaic | how are mosaics made", "A **mosaic** is a picture made from many small pieces of colored stone, glass or tile, called **tesserae**, set into cement or plaster. Ancient Romans decorated floors with mosaics, and Byzantine churches have dazzling gold glass mosaics."],
  ["what is pastel art | oil pastels vs soft pastels", "**Soft pastels** are sticks of powdery pigment that blend easily with your fingers and create soft, velvety colors. **Oil pastels** are mixed with oil and wax, so they're creamy, bright and less dusty, but harder to blend. Degas made many famous pastel drawings."],
  ["what is charcoal drawing | how to draw with charcoal", "**Charcoal** is burnt wood used for drawing, and it makes rich, dark blacks and smudgy grays. Vine charcoal is soft and easy to erase, while compressed charcoal is darker. A kneaded eraser can lift charcoal to make highlights. Spray it with fixative so it doesn't smear."],
  ["what do pencil numbers mean | what is a 2b pencil | hb vs 2b pencil", "Drawing pencils are graded by hardness. **H** pencils are hard and make light, thin lines, while **B** pencils are soft and make dark, smudgy lines; the higher the number, the stronger the effect. **HB** is in the middle, like a regular school pencil, and artists often sketch with 2B to 6B."],
  ["what is calligraphy | how to start calligraphy", "**Calligraphy** is the art of beautiful handwriting. Chinese and Japanese calligraphy uses brushes and ink, Arabic calligraphy creates flowing decorative script, and Western calligraphy often uses a broad-edged pen. Beginners can start with a brush pen, practicing basic strokes slowly, with thin upstrokes and thick downstrokes."],
  ["what is printmaking | types of printmaking", "**Printmaking** is creating art by transferring ink from a prepared surface, such as a block, plate or screen, onto paper, so you can make many copies. Main types include **relief** (woodcut and linocut), **intaglio** (etching and engraving), **lithography** and **screen printing**."],
  ["what is a woodcut | what is a linocut | how do you make a block print", "In a **woodcut** or **linocut**, you carve away parts of a wooden block or linoleum sheet. The raised parts that remain get rolled with ink and pressed onto paper, while carved areas stay white. Remember the image prints in reverse, so letters must be carved backwards!"],
  ["what is screen printing | how does screen printing work", "**Screen printing** pushes ink through a fine mesh screen with a squeegee. A stencil blocks the ink in some areas, so it only passes through the design. Each color needs its own screen. It's used for T-shirts and posters, and Andy Warhol used it for his art."],
  ["what is etching in art | what is engraving", "In **etching**, an artist scratches a drawing through a waxy coating on a metal plate, then dips it in acid, which bites lines into the exposed metal. In **engraving**, the lines are cut directly into the metal with a sharp tool. Ink is wiped into the lines and pressed onto paper. Rembrandt was a brilliant etcher."],
  ["how is pottery made | what is ceramics", "**Pottery** (ceramics) is made by shaping clay, letting it dry, then **firing** it in a very hot oven called a **kiln**, which turns it hard and permanent. Clay can be shaped by hand or on a spinning potter's wheel. Pottery is one of humanity's oldest crafts, going back at least 20,000 years."],
  ["what is a kiln | what is glaze in pottery", "A **kiln** is a special oven that heats pottery to very high temperatures, often over 1,000 °C (1,800 °F), turning soft clay into hard ceramic. **Glaze** is a coating made of glassy minerals that melts in the kiln to form a shiny, colorful and often waterproof surface."],
  ["what is a pottery wheel | how do you throw a pot", "A **pottery wheel** is a spinning disc used to shape round pots, which potters call **throwing**. You first **center** the wet clay so it spins evenly, then push your thumbs in to open it and gently pull the walls up. It takes lots of practice, so expect wobbly pots at first!"],
  ["how to make a pinch pot | what are coil pots and slab pottery", "Three classic hand-building methods are **pinch pots** (pressing your thumb into a ball of clay and pinching the walls out), **coil pots** (stacking long rolled snakes of clay) and **slab building** (cutting flat sheets of clay and joining them). Join pieces by scratching the surfaces and adding watery clay called **slip**."],
  ["types of clay | earthenware vs stoneware vs porcelain", "**Earthenware** is fired at lower temperatures and stays a bit porous, like terracotta flowerpots. **Stoneware** is fired hotter, so it's strong and waterproof, great for mugs. **Porcelain** is made from fine white clay fired very hot, making it delicate, smooth and sometimes see-through; it was first perfected in China."],
  ["how are bronze statues made | what is lost wax casting", "Many bronze statues are made with **lost-wax casting**. The artist makes a model, then a wax copy of it, which is covered in a heat-proof mold. The mold is heated so the wax melts and drains out (\"lost\"), then molten bronze is poured into the empty space. When it cools, the mold is broken away."],
  ["what are the types of sculpture | what is relief sculpture | carving vs modeling", "Sculptors can **carve** (cutting away from stone or wood), **model** (building up soft material like clay), **cast** (pouring liquid metal or plaster into a mold) or **assemble** (joining found objects). A **relief** sculpture sticks out from a flat background, like the faces on coins, while a free-standing sculpture can be seen from all sides."],
  ["what is origami | where does origami come from", "**Origami** is the Japanese art of folding paper into shapes like cranes, frogs and boxes, traditionally without cutting or gluing. A legend says folding 1,000 paper cranes grants a wish. Origami ideas are even used by engineers to fold up solar panels for space."],
  ["what is stained glass | how is stained glass made", "**Stained glass** is colored glass cut into shapes and joined with strips of lead to make pictures, often in windows. Light shining through makes the colors glow. Medieval cathedrals like Chartres in France are famous for their stained glass, which also told Bible stories to people who couldn't read."],
  ["what is analogous color | what are color schemes in art", "A **color scheme** is a planned set of colors. **Analogous** colors sit next to each other on the color wheel, like yellow, yellow-orange and orange, and look calm together. A **complementary** scheme uses opposites for contrast, a **triadic** scheme uses three evenly spaced colors and a **monochromatic** scheme uses tints and shades of just one color."],
  ["what is hue and saturation | hue saturation value meaning", "**Hue** is the name of a color, like red or blue. **Saturation** (or intensity) is how pure and vivid it is versus dull and grayish. **Value** is how light or dark it is. Together they describe any color, and many digital art programs use these settings."],
  ["what do colors mean in art | color psychology for artists", "Artists use colors to create moods. Warm colors like red and orange can feel energetic, exciting or angry, while cool colors like blue and green often feel calm, sad or peaceful. Meanings vary by culture, too: white stands for weddings in some places and mourning in others."],
  ["how does a camera work | photography basics", "A camera lets light in through a **lens**, which focuses it onto a light-sensitive surface, either film or a digital **sensor**. The **aperture** controls how much light comes in, and the **shutter** controls how long it comes in for. The result is a captured picture of that moment."],
  ["what is iso in photography", "These three settings form the **exposure triangle**. **Aperture** is the size of the lens opening; a wider one lets in more light and blurs the background. **Shutter speed** is how long the shutter stays open; fast speeds freeze motion, slow ones blur it. **ISO** is the sensor's sensitivity to light; higher ISO helps in dim light but adds graininess."],
  ["tips for taking good photos | photography composition tips", "Try the **rule of thirds**, placing your subject off-center. Get closer than you think, watch the background so nothing sprouts from people's heads, and use natural light from a window or outdoors. Try unusual angles, like crouching low or shooting from above, and keep the camera steady."],
  ["what is depth of field | how to blur the background in a photo", "**Depth of field** is how much of a photo, from near to far, looks sharp. A **shallow** depth of field keeps only your subject sharp and blurs the background, which you get with a wide aperture, a close subject or portrait mode on phones. A **deep** depth of field keeps everything sharp, great for landscapes."],
  ["what is a camera obscura | what is a pinhole camera", "A **camera obscura** (\"dark room\") is a dark room or box with a tiny hole in one side. Light passing through the hole projects an upside-down image of the outside scene onto the opposite wall. A **pinhole camera** uses the same idea with a box and film or photo paper, and it needs no lens."],
  ["who invented photography | what was the first photograph", "The oldest surviving camera photograph was made by Frenchman **Joseph Nicéphore Niépce** around 1826–1827, a view from his window that needed hours of exposure. His partner **Louis Daguerre** invented the daguerreotype in 1839, and **William Henry Fox Talbot** in England developed a negative process that allowed many prints from one image."],
  ["film vs digital photography | how does film photography work", "**Film** cameras capture images on a strip of plastic coated with light-sensitive chemicals, which must be developed before you can see the pictures. **Digital** cameras record light with an electronic sensor made of millions of pixels and save the image as a file you can see immediately."],
  ["how does animation work | how do cartoons move", "**Animation** shows a series of still pictures, called **frames**, one after another very quickly. Each picture is slightly different from the last, and our brains blend them into smooth motion. Movies usually show 24 frames every second."],
  ["what is a flipbook | how to make a flipbook", "A **flipbook** is a little book of drawings that seems to move when you flip the pages fast. To make one, draw a simple picture on the last page of a small notepad, then on each page before it, draw it again with a tiny change. Flip from back to front and watch it come alive!"],
  ["what is stop motion animation | what is claymation", "**Stop motion** animation is made by photographing real objects, moving them a tiny bit, taking another photo and repeating hundreds or thousands of times. When played quickly, the objects appear to move on their own. **Claymation** is stop motion using clay figures, like the *Wallace & Gromit* films."],
  ["what is 2d animation vs 3d animation | what is cgi animation", "**2D animation** uses flat drawings, traditionally hand-drawn on paper or clear sheets called cels, and now often drawn digitally. **3D animation** (CGI) builds characters as digital models inside a computer that can be posed, lit and filmed from any angle. *Toy Story* (1995) was the first full-length movie made entirely with computer animation."],
  ["what are keyframes in animation | what is in-betweening", "**Keyframes** are the most important drawings or poses in a movement, like the start and end of a jump. **In-betweens** are the drawings that fill the gaps between keyframes to make the motion smooth. In big studios, lead animators draw keyframes and assistants draw the in-betweens; in computer animation, software can help fill them in."],
  ["what are the 12 principles of animation | what is squash and stretch", "The **12 principles of animation** were described by Disney animators Ollie Johnston and Frank Thomas. They include **squash and stretch** (objects squish and stretch to look flexible and alive, like a bouncing ball), **anticipation**, **timing**, **exaggeration**, **follow-through**, **arcs** and **appeal**. Animators still learn them today."],
  ["what is rotoscoping", "**Rotoscoping** is an animation technique where artists trace over live-action film frame by frame to create realistic movement. It was invented by Max Fleischer around 1915 and has been used in classic cartoons and modern films and music videos."],
  ["what was the first animated movie | first full length animated film", "Disney's ***Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs*** (1937) is famous as the first full-length cel-animated feature film in color and a huge success. A few earlier animated features were made in Argentina and Germany, such as Lotte Reiniger's silhouette film *The Adventures of Prince Achmed* (1926)."],
  ["tell me about shakespeare", "**William Shakespeare** (1564–1616) was an English playwright and poet, often called the greatest writer in the English language. He wrote about 37 plays, including comedies, tragedies and histories, plus 154 sonnets. He also invented or first recorded many words and phrases we still use, like \"bedroom,\" \"eyeball\" and \"wild-goose chase.\""],
  ["what is romeo and juliet about | romeo and juliet summary", "***Romeo and Juliet*** is Shakespeare's tragedy about two teenagers in Verona, Italy, who fall in love even though their families, the Montagues and the Capulets, are bitter enemies. They secretly marry, but a chain of fights, misunderstandings and bad timing leads to a sad ending. Their loss finally makes the families make peace."],
  ["what is hamlet about | hamlet summary", "***Hamlet*** is Shakespeare's tragedy about a prince of Denmark whose father, the king, has died. The king's ghost tells Hamlet he was murdered by his brother Claudius, who has taken the throne and married Hamlet's mother. Hamlet struggles with doubt and delay as he seeks the truth, and the famous line \"To be, or not to be\" comes from this play."],
  ["what is macbeth about | macbeth summary", "***Macbeth*** is Shakespeare's tragedy about a Scottish nobleman who meets three witches who predict he will become king. Pushed on by ambition and his wife, Lady Macbeth, he seizes the crown by terrible means. Guilt and fear then destroy them both, showing how unchecked ambition can ruin a person."],
  ["what is a midsummer night's dream about | midsummer night's dream summary", "***A Midsummer Night's Dream*** is Shakespeare's magical comedy. Four young lovers run into an enchanted forest, where the mischievous fairy **Puck** uses a love potion and mixes up who loves whom. Meanwhile, a group of amateur actors rehearse a play, and one of them, Bottom, has his head turned into a donkey's! Everything ends happily."],
  ["what is the tempest about | the tempest shakespeare summary", "***The Tempest*** is one of Shakespeare's last plays. Prospero, a magician and rightful Duke of Milan, lives on a remote island with his daughter Miranda after being betrayed by his brother. When his enemies sail nearby, he uses magic and the spirit Ariel to cause a storm that shipwrecks them, leading to forgiveness, romance and Prospero giving up his magic."],
  ["what is julius caesar the play about | julius caesar shakespeare summary", "Shakespeare's ***Julius Caesar*** is about a group of Roman senators, including Brutus and Cassius, who fear Caesar is becoming too powerful and plot against him. After the deed, Mark Antony gives a famous speech beginning \"Friends, Romans, countrymen, lend me your ears\" that turns the crowd, and civil war follows. It explores loyalty, honor and power."],
  ["what is twelfth night about | twelfth night summary", "***Twelfth Night*** is a Shakespeare comedy about twins, Viola and Sebastian, separated in a shipwreck. Viola disguises herself as a young man and works for Duke Orsino, whom she falls for, while the countess Olivia falls for Viola in disguise. When Sebastian turns up, hilarious mix-ups follow before everything is sorted out."],
  ["what is king lear about | king lear summary", "***King Lear*** is Shakespeare's tragedy about an old king who divides his kingdom among his daughters based on how much they flatter him. He rejects his honest youngest daughter, Cordelia, and trusts the two who flatter him, who then turn against him. It's a powerful story about pride, family and learning what love truly is."],
  ["what is pride and prejudice about", "**Jane Austen** (1775–1817) was an English novelist known for witty stories about love, manners and family life. In ***Pride and Prejudice*** (1813), lively Elizabeth Bennet and the proud Mr. Darcy dislike each other at first, but as they overcome their pride and their prejudices, they realize they've fallen in love."],
  ["what is jane eyre about | jane eyre summary", "***Jane Eyre*** (1847) by **Charlotte Brontë** follows an orphan girl who survives a harsh childhood and becomes a governess at Thornfield Hall. She falls in love with its mysterious owner, Mr. Rochester, but discovers a dark secret in the house. Jane always stays true to her own sense of right and her independence."],
  ["what is wuthering heights about | wuthering heights summary", "***Wuthering Heights*** (1847) by **Emily Brontë** is a dark, stormy story set on the Yorkshire moors. It tells of the intense bond between Catherine Earnshaw and Heathcliff, an orphan taken in by her family, and how jealousy and revenge affect two families across two generations."],
  ["what is little women about | little women summary", "***Little Women*** (1868–1869) by **Louisa May Alcott** follows the four March sisters, Meg, Jo, Beth and Amy, growing up in Massachusetts during the American Civil War while their father is away. Tomboyish Jo dreams of being a writer. It's a warm story about family, kindness and growing up."],
  ["tell me about dickens", "**Charles Dickens** (1812–1870) was the most popular English novelist of the Victorian era. Having worked in a factory as a child, he wrote stories that exposed poverty and injustice, full of memorable characters. His books include *Oliver Twist*, *A Christmas Carol*, *Great Expectations* and *A Tale of Two Cities*."],
  ["what is a christmas carol about | who is ebenezer scrooge", "***A Christmas Carol*** (1843) by Charles Dickens is about **Ebenezer Scrooge**, a grumpy, stingy old man who hates Christmas. On Christmas Eve he is visited by the ghost of his old partner and then three spirits: the Ghosts of Christmas Past, Present and Yet to Come. Seeing his life this way, he becomes generous and kind, especially to the Cratchit family and Tiny Tim."],
  ["what is oliver twist about | oliver twist summary", "***Oliver Twist*** (1837–1839) by Charles Dickens follows an orphan boy who is raised in a harsh workhouse, where he famously asks, \"Please, sir, I want some more.\" He runs away to London and falls in with a gang of young pickpockets led by Fagin, before kind people help him find a safe home and the truth about his family."],
  ["what is great expectations about | great expectations summary", "***Great Expectations*** (1860–1861) by Charles Dickens follows **Pip**, a poor orphan who receives money from a secret benefactor to become a gentleman in London. Along the way he meets the strange Miss Havisham, who still wears her old wedding dress, and learns that loyalty and kindness matter more than wealth and status."],
  ["what is a tale of two cities about", "***A Tale of Two Cities*** (1859) by Charles Dickens is set in London and Paris during the French Revolution. It follows characters caught up in the turmoil and ends with an act of great self-sacrifice. It opens with the famous line \"It was the best of times, it was the worst of times.\""],
  ["what is moby dick about | moby dick summary", "***Moby-Dick*** (1851) by **Herman Melville** is narrated by a sailor who says \"Call me Ishmael.\" He joins the whaling ship *Pequod*, whose captain, Ahab, is obsessed with hunting the giant white whale that once took his leg. It's a sea adventure and a story about how obsession can lead to disaster."],
  ["what is tom sawyer about", "**Mark Twain** (1835–1910), the pen name of Samuel Clemens, was a great American humorist and author. ***The Adventures of Tom Sawyer*** (1876) follows a clever, mischievous boy growing up along the Mississippi River, who tricks his friends into whitewashing a fence for him, hunts for treasure and gets mixed up in a mystery."],
  ["what is huckleberry finn about | huckleberry finn summary", "***Adventures of Huckleberry Finn*** (1884) by Mark Twain follows Huck, a boy who escapes his rough father and rafts down the Mississippi River with Jim, a man escaping slavery. Through their adventures, Huck learns to see Jim as a true friend and decides to do what's right, even when society says otherwise."],
  ["what is treasure island about | treasure island summary", "***Treasure Island*** (1883) by **Robert Louis Stevenson** is a pirate adventure. Young Jim Hawkins finds a treasure map and sets sail to find buried gold, but the ship's charming cook, **Long John Silver**, turns out to be a pirate leader. The book gave us many pirate ideas, like maps with an \"X\" marking the spot."],
  ["what is dr jekyll and mr hyde about | jekyll and hyde summary", "***Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde*** (1886) by Robert Louis Stevenson is about a respectable doctor who creates a potion that transforms him into Mr. Hyde, a cruel version of himself. The story explores the good and bad sides of human nature, and \"Jekyll and Hyde\" is now a phrase for someone with two very different personalities."],
  ["what is frankenstein about", "***Frankenstein*** (1818) was written by **Mary Shelley**, who started it when she was just 18. Scientist Victor Frankenstein builds a creature and brings it to life, then runs away in fear. The lonely creature longs for friendship and is hurt by rejection. Fun fact: Frankenstein is the scientist's name, not the creature's! It's often called the first science-fiction novel."],
  ["what is dracula about | who wrote dracula", "***Dracula*** (1897) by Irish author **Bram Stoker** is a classic spooky novel told through letters and diary entries. A young lawyer visits Count Dracula's castle in Transylvania and discovers he is a vampire, and a group led by Professor Van Helsing works together to stop him. It made vampires famous in stories and films."],
  ["what is alice in wonderland about", "***Alice's Adventures in Wonderland*** (1865) by **Lewis Carroll** begins when Alice follows a White Rabbit down a rabbit hole into a topsy-turvy world. She shrinks and grows, attends a Mad Tea Party with the Mad Hatter, meets the grinning Cheshire Cat and plays croquet with the Queen of Hearts, who shouts \"Off with their heads!\" The sequel is *Through the Looking-Glass*."],
  ["what is peter pan about | who wrote peter pan", "***Peter Pan*** by Scottish writer **J.M. Barrie**, first a 1904 play and later a novel, is about a boy who never grows up. He flies with the fairy **Tinker Bell** to the Darling children's window and takes Wendy, John and Michael to **Neverland**, where they meet the Lost Boys, mermaids and the villainous Captain Hook."],
  ["what is the wizard of oz about | the wonderful wizard of oz summary", "***The Wonderful Wizard of Oz*** (1900) by **L. Frank Baum** is about Dorothy, a girl from Kansas whose house is carried by a cyclone to the magical Land of Oz. She follows the yellow brick road with the Scarecrow, the Tin Woodman and the Cowardly Lion to ask the Wizard for help getting home. In the book her magic shoes are silver; they became ruby red in the 1939 film."],
  ["what is the wind in the willows about", "***The Wind in the Willows*** (1908) by **Kenneth Grahame** follows gentle Mole, kind Ratty, wise Badger and the boastful, car-crazy **Mr. Toad**, who keeps getting into trouble. It's a cozy story about friendship and the English countryside along the river."],
  ["what is the jungle book about | who wrote the jungle book", "***The Jungle Book*** (1894) by **Rudyard Kipling** is a collection of stories set in India. The most famous ones follow **Mowgli**, a boy raised by wolves, who learns the law of the jungle from Baloo the bear and Bagheera the panther and must face the fierce tiger Shere Khan. It also includes the story of the brave mongoose Rikki-Tikki-Tavi."],
  ["what is the secret garden about | the secret garden summary", "***The Secret Garden*** (1911) by **Frances Hodgson Burnett** follows Mary Lennox, a spoiled, lonely girl sent to live in her uncle's gloomy mansion in England. She discovers a hidden, locked garden and secretly brings it back to life with her friend Dickon and her sickly cousin Colin. As the garden blooms, the children grow happier and healthier too."],
  ["what is anne of green gables about", "***Anne of Green Gables*** (1908) by Canadian author **L.M. Montgomery** is about Anne Shirley, a talkative, imaginative red-haired orphan who is sent by mistake to an elderly brother and sister who wanted a boy to help on their farm on Prince Edward Island. Anne wins their hearts and has many funny adventures growing up."],
  ["what is heidi about | heidi book summary", "***Heidi*** (1881) by Swiss author **Johanna Spyri** is about a cheerful orphan girl who goes to live with her grumpy grandfather in the Swiss Alps. She loves the mountains, the goats and her friend Peter. When she's taken to the city of Frankfurt, she befriends Clara, a girl who uses a wheelchair, and later the mountain air helps Clara grow stronger."],
  ["what is black beauty about | who wrote black beauty", "***Black Beauty*** (1877) by **Anna Sewell** is told by a horse describing his life, from a happy childhood in a meadow to working for kind and cruel owners in Victorian England. It showed readers how animals feel and helped improve the treatment of horses."],
  ["what is pinocchio about | who wrote pinocchio", "***The Adventures of Pinocchio*** (1883) by Italian writer **Carlo Collodi** is about a wooden puppet carved by the woodcarver Geppetto who comes to life. Pinocchio's nose grows when he lies, and he gets into lots of trouble before learning to be honest, brave and kind, and finally becomes a real boy."],
  ["what is gulliver's travels about | gullivers travels summary", "***Gulliver's Travels*** (1726) by **Jonathan Swift** follows Lemuel Gulliver, a ship's surgeon who visits strange lands. In **Lilliput** the people are only about 15 cm (6 inches) tall, and in Brobdingnag they're giants. Swift used these adventures to poke fun at politics and human foolishness."],
  ["what is robinson crusoe about", "***Robinson Crusoe*** (1719) by **Daniel Defoe** is about a sailor shipwrecked alone on a remote island for many years. He builds a shelter, farms, tames animals and keeps a journal. Later he meets a man he names Friday, who becomes his companion. It's often considered one of the first English novels."],
  ["what is don quixote about", "***Don Quixote*** (1605 and 1615) by Spanish writer **Miguel de Cervantes** is about an old gentleman who reads so many tales of knights that he decides to become one. With his loyal, down-to-earth squire Sancho Panza, he rides out on adventures, famously attacking windmills he thinks are giants. It's often called the first modern novel."],
  ["what is the three musketeers about", "***The Three Musketeers*** (1844) by French author **Alexandre Dumas** follows young d'Artagnan, who travels to Paris to join the king's Musketeers and befriends three of them: Athos, Porthos and Aramis. Together they have sword fights and adventures, defending the queen against the schemes of Cardinal Richelieu. Their motto is \"All for one, one for all!\""],
  ["what is the count of monte cristo about", "***The Count of Monte Cristo*** (1844) by Alexandre Dumas is about Edmond Dantès, a young sailor wrongly imprisoned in an island fortress because of jealous rivals. After many years he escapes, finds a hidden treasure and returns as the mysterious, wealthy Count of Monte Cristo to bring his enemies to justice."],
  ["what is les miserables about | les miserables summary", "***Les Misérables*** (1862) by **Victor Hugo** follows Jean Valjean, a former prisoner who stole a loaf of bread, as he builds a new life of kindness after a bishop shows him mercy. He is pursued for years by the strict police inspector Javert and raises an orphan girl, Cosette, with the story reaching its climax during an 1832 uprising in Paris. It became a famous musical."],
  ["what is the hunchback of notre dame about", "***The Hunchback of Notre-Dame*** (1831) by Victor Hugo is set in medieval Paris. Quasimodo, the kind-hearted bell-ringer of Notre-Dame Cathedral, is mocked for his appearance, and he protects Esmeralda, a young woman who showed him kindness. The book also helped inspire efforts to preserve old buildings like the cathedral."],
  ["what is twenty thousand leagues under the sea about", "**Jules Verne** (1828–1905) was a French author often called a \"father of science fiction.\" In ***Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Sea*** (1870), a professor and his companions are captured aboard the amazing submarine *Nautilus*, commanded by the mysterious **Captain Nemo**, and travel the world's oceans. Verne also wrote *Journey to the Center of the Earth*."],
  ["what is around the world in 80 days about | around the world in eighty days summary", "In Jules Verne's ***Around the World in Eighty Days*** (1872), a precise English gentleman named **Phileas Fogg** bets his fortune that he can travel around the globe in 80 days. With his servant Passepartout, he races by train, steamship and even elephant, while a detective chases him by mistake. A clever twist about time zones decides the bet!"],
  ["what is the time machine about | what is the war of the worlds about", "**H.G. Wells** (1866–1946) was an English writer and science-fiction pioneer. In ***The Time Machine*** (1895), an inventor travels far into the future and finds humanity has split into two strange groups. In ***The War of the Worlds*** (1898), Martians invade England, but are stopped in a surprising way: by tiny germs."],
  ["who was sherlock holmes | what is the hound of the baskervilles about", "**Sherlock Holmes** is a brilliant fictional detective created by **Arthur Conan Doyle** in 1887. He lives at 221B Baker Street in London and solves mysteries with sharp observation and logic, helped by his friend Dr. Watson. In ***The Hound of the Baskervilles***, he investigates a legend about a ghostly hound haunting a family on the misty moors."],
  ["what is the call of the wild about | who was jack london", "**Jack London** (1876–1916) was an American adventure writer. In ***The Call of the Wild*** (1903), Buck, a big pet dog in California, is taken to the Yukon during the gold rush to pull sleds. He learns to survive the harsh north and eventually answers the call of the wild to run with the wolves. In *White Fang*, a wolf-dog makes the opposite journey."],
  ["what is the hobbit about | the hobbit summary", "***The Hobbit*** (1937) by **J.R.R. Tolkien** follows **Bilbo Baggins**, a comfortable hobbit who is swept off by the wizard Gandalf and thirteen dwarves on a quest to win back their treasure from the dragon **Smaug**. Along the way he outwits trolls and finds a magic ring in a riddle contest with Gollum, and discovers he's braver than he thought."],
  ["what is the lord of the rings about | lord of the rings summary", "***The Lord of the Rings*** (1954–1955) by J.R.R. Tolkien is an epic fantasy in which the hobbit **Frodo Baggins** must destroy the One Ring, a powerful evil ring made by the Dark Lord Sauron, by throwing it into the fires of Mount Doom. With the Fellowship, including Sam, Gandalf, Aragorn, Legolas and Gimli, he faces a long, dangerous journey."],
  ["what is the lion the witch and the wardrobe about | what is narnia", "***The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe*** (1950) by **C.S. Lewis** is about four siblings who step through a wardrobe into **Narnia**, a magical land frozen in endless winter by the White Witch. They join the great lion **Aslan** to free Narnia. It's the most famous of the seven *Chronicles of Narnia* books."],
  ["what is charlotte's web about | charlottes web summary", "***Charlotte's Web*** (1952) by **E.B. White** is about Wilbur, a pig on a farm, and his friend Charlotte, a clever spider. When Wilbur is in danger, Charlotte spins words like \"SOME PIG\" into her web to show everyone he's special. It's a touching story about friendship and the cycle of life."],
  ["what is charlie and the chocolate factory about", "**Roald Dahl** (1916–1990) was a British author of funny, wildly imaginative children's books like *Matilda*, *The BFG* and *James and the Giant Peach*. In ***Charlie and the Chocolate Factory*** (1964), poor but kind Charlie Bucket finds a Golden Ticket to tour Willy Wonka's magical factory, where the other badly behaved children meet silly fates."],
  ["what is matilda about | matilda book summary", "***Matilda*** (1988) by Roald Dahl is about a brilliant little girl who loves books but has neglectful parents. At school she faces the terrifying headmistress Miss Trunchbull, but finds a friend in her kind teacher Miss Honey. Matilda discovers she has special powers and uses them to set things right."],
  ["what is winnie the pooh about | who wrote winnie the pooh", "***Winnie-the-Pooh*** (1926) by **A.A. Milne** tells gentle stories about a honey-loving \"bear of very little brain\" and his friends in the Hundred Acre Wood, including Piglet, Eeyore, Tigger, Rabbit, Owl and Kanga. They're based on the toys of Milne's son, Christopher Robin."],
  ["what is the little prince about", "***The Little Prince*** (1943) by French pilot and author **Antoine de Saint-Exupéry** is about a pilot stranded in the desert who meets a little prince from a tiny asteroid. The prince describes visiting other planets and his love for a rose. A fox teaches him that \"what is essential is invisible to the eye.\""],
  ["what is the iliad about | iliad summary", "The ***Iliad***, an ancient Greek epic poem credited to **Homer**, is set during the final year of the Trojan War. It focuses on the anger of the great warrior **Achilles**, who quarrels with King Agamemnon and refuses to fight, and on the Trojan hero Hector. The gods take sides and interfere throughout."],
  ["what is the odyssey about | odyssey summary", "The ***Odyssey***, an epic poem credited to **Homer**, follows the clever Greek hero **Odysseus** on his ten-year journey home from the Trojan War. He outwits the one-eyed Cyclops, resists the Sirens' song, escapes the enchantress Circe and finally returns to Ithaca, where his faithful wife Penelope and son Telemachus have waited for him."],
  ["who was homer the poet", "**Homer** is the name given to the ancient Greek poet credited with the *Iliad* and the *Odyssey*, probably composed around the 700s BC. We know almost nothing about his life, and some scholars think the poems grew from many generations of storytellers. Legend says he was blind."],
  ["what is beowulf about | beowulf summary", "***Beowulf*** is an epic poem written in Old English over 1,000 years ago. The hero Beowulf comes to help the Danish king, whose great hall is being attacked by a monster called **Grendel**. Beowulf defeats Grendel and then Grendel's mother, and years later, as an old king, he faces a fire-breathing dragon."],
  ["what is the canterbury tales about | who wrote the canterbury tales", "***The Canterbury Tales*** was written by **Geoffrey Chaucer** in the late 1300s. A group of pilgrims traveling to Canterbury hold a storytelling contest to pass the time, and each one tells a tale. The characters, like the Knight, the Miller and the Wife of Bath, give a lively picture of medieval English life."],
  ["what is the divine comedy about", "***The Divine Comedy*** is a long poem by Italian poet **Dante Alighieri**, finished around 1320. Dante imagines journeying through the afterlife in three parts, Inferno, Purgatorio and Paradiso, guided first by the Roman poet Virgil and then by Beatrice. It's considered one of the greatest works of world literature."],
  ["what is one thousand and one nights | what is arabian nights", "***One Thousand and One Nights*** (also called *The Arabian Nights*) is a collection of Middle Eastern and South Asian folk tales. In the frame story, the clever **Scheherazade** tells a king a new story every night, stopping at an exciting part so he'll want to hear more. Later collections added tales like \"Aladdin,\" \"Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves\" and \"Sinbad the Sailor.\""],
  ["what is journey to the west | who is the monkey king", "***Journey to the West*** is a classic Chinese novel from the 1500s. It follows the monk Tang Sanzang traveling to India to bring back holy scriptures, protected by **Sun Wukong, the Monkey King**, who can leap thousands of miles in a somersault and change into 72 forms, plus the greedy Pigsy and the quiet Sandy."],
  ["what is the ramayana | what is the mahabharata", "The ***Ramayana*** and ***Mahabharata*** are ancient Sanskrit epics from India. The Ramayana tells of Prince **Rama**, who, with his brother Lakshmana and the monkey hero Hanuman, rescues his wife Sita from the demon king Ravana. The Mahabharata, one of the longest poems in the world, tells of a great struggle between two branches of a royal family and contains the Bhagavad Gita."],
  ["what is to kill a mockingbird about | to kill a mockingbird summary", "***To Kill a Mockingbird*** (1960) by **Harper Lee** is told by Scout Finch, a young girl in a small Alabama town in the 1930s. Her father, the lawyer **Atticus Finch**, defends a Black man falsely accused of a crime, standing up against racism in their town. Scout learns about courage, empathy and justice."],
  ["what is the old man and the sea about", "***The Old Man and the Sea*** (1952) by **Ernest Hemingway** is about Santiago, an old Cuban fisherman who hasn't caught a fish in 84 days. He sails far out and hooks an enormous marlin, battling it for days. It's a short, powerful story about determination and dignity."],
  ["what is the great gatsby about | the great gatsby summary", "***The Great Gatsby*** (1925) by **F. Scott Fitzgerald** is set in 1920s New York during the Jazz Age. The narrator, Nick, gets to know his mysterious, wealthy neighbor **Jay Gatsby**, who throws huge parties hoping to win back Daisy, his old love. It explores dreams, wealth and the idea of the American Dream."],
  ["what is animal farm about | animal farm summary", "***Animal Farm*** (1945) by **George Orwell** is a fable in which farm animals rebel against their farmer to create a fair society where \"All animals are equal.\" Over time, the pigs who lead them become just as bad as the humans, changing the rule to \"some animals are more equal than others.\" It's a warning about power and corruption."],
  ["what is 1984 about | what is nineteen eighty-four about", "***Nineteen Eighty-Four*** (1949) by George Orwell imagines a future country ruled by a controlling government led by \"Big Brother,\" who watches everyone through screens. Winston Smith, who works rewriting history, begins to secretly question the system. The book gave us terms like \"Big Brother\" and \"thought police.\""],
  ["what is lord of the flies about", "***Lord of the Flies*** (1954) by **William Golding** is about a group of schoolboys stranded on an island after a plane crash. They try to govern themselves, led by Ralph, with a conch shell used to call meetings, but rivalries and fear soon break their society apart. It explores what happens when rules disappear."],
  ["what is fahrenheit 451 about", "***Fahrenheit 451*** (1953) by **Ray Bradbury** is set in a future where books are banned, and \"firemen\" burn them instead of putting out fires. Fireman Guy Montag begins to wonder what's inside the books and starts to rebel. The title is said to be the temperature at which paper catches fire."],
  ["what is the diary of anne frank about", "**Anne Frank** was a Jewish girl who hid with her family in a secret annex in Amsterdam for two years during World War II. Her diary, published as ***The Diary of a Young Girl***, describes daily life in hiding, her hopes and her thoughts about growing up. She did not survive the war, and her diary became one of the most-read books in the world."],
  ["what is things fall apart about | who was chinua achebe", "***Things Fall Apart*** (1958) by Nigerian author **Chinua Achebe** follows Okonkwo, a proud and respected man in an Igbo village, and shows how his community changes when European missionaries and colonial rulers arrive. It's one of the most widely read African novels in the world."],
  ["what is a wrinkle in time about", "***A Wrinkle in Time*** (1962) by **Madeleine L'Engle** follows Meg Murry, her little brother Charles Wallace and their friend Calvin as they travel through space and time using a \"tesseract\" to rescue Meg's scientist father from an evil force. Meg learns that her faults can also be her strengths."],
  ["what is the phantom tollbooth about", "***The Phantom Tollbooth*** (1961) by **Norton Juster** is about Milo, a bored boy who finds a magic tollbooth in his room. He drives through it into the Lands Beyond, visiting Dictionopolis (land of words) and Digitopolis (land of numbers), with a watchdog named Tock. It's full of wordplay and jokes."],
  ["what is the giver about | the giver summary", "***The Giver*** (1993) by **Lois Lowry** is set in a community with no pain, no choices and no colors. When 12-year-old Jonas is chosen to be the Receiver of Memory, an old man called the Giver shares memories of the past with him, including color, music and love, and Jonas realizes what his community has lost."],
  ["what is hatchet about | hatchet book summary", "***Hatchet*** (1987) by **Gary Paulsen** is about 13-year-old Brian, who survives a small plane crash in the Canadian wilderness with only a hatchet his mother gave him. He learns to make fire, find food and build a shelter, growing patient and strong as he waits to be rescued."],
  ["what is holes about | holes book summary", "***Holes*** (1998) by **Louis Sachar** follows Stanley Yelnats, a boy sent to Camp Green Lake, a dry desert camp, for a crime he didn't commit. The boys are forced to dig holes every day, supposedly to build character. Stanley uncovers a mystery linking his family's \"curse,\" buried treasure and the camp's past."],
  ["what is harry potter about | who wrote harry potter", "The ***Harry Potter*** series is seven fantasy books by British author **J.K. Rowling**, the first published in 1997. Harry discovers on his 11th birthday that he's a wizard and goes to Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry, where he makes friends with Ron and Hermione and faces the dark wizard Voldemort."],
  ["what is percy jackson about | percy jackson greek mythology", "The ***Percy Jackson and the Olympians*** series by **Rick Riordan**, starting with *The Lightning Thief* (2005), is about a boy who discovers he's the son of Poseidon, the Greek sea god. At Camp Half-Blood he trains with other demigods and goes on quests full of monsters and gods from Greek mythology. It's a fun way to learn the myths."],
  ["what is mary poppins about | who wrote mary poppins", "***Mary Poppins*** (1934) by **P.L. Travers** is about a magical, no-nonsense nanny who blows in on the east wind to look after the Banks children in London. She slides up banisters, has tea on the ceiling and takes the children on amazing adventures, then leaves when the wind changes."],
  ["what is the velveteen rabbit about", "***The Velveteen Rabbit*** (1922) by **Margery Williams** is about a stuffed toy rabbit who wants to become real. The wise old Skin Horse explains that a toy becomes Real when a child loves it for a long, long time. Through the boy's love, the rabbit's wish comes true."],
  ["what is swiss family robinson about", "***The Swiss Family Robinson*** (1812) by **Johann David Wyss** is about a Swiss family shipwrecked on a deserted tropical island. Working together, they build a home in a giant tree, farm, tame animals and use their cleverness to survive and thrive."],
  ["what is peter rabbit about", "**Beatrix Potter** (1866–1943) was an English author, illustrator and naturalist. In ***The Tale of Peter Rabbit*** (1902), naughty Peter disobeys his mother and sneaks into Mr. McGregor's garden, losing his jacket and shoes as he escapes. She wrote and painted over 20 little books about animals."],
  ["what is the cat in the hat about", "**Dr. Seuss** was the pen name of **Theodor Geisel** (1904–1991), an American author famous for silly rhymes and wacky creatures. In ***The Cat in the Hat*** (1957), a mischievous cat turns two children's rainy day into chaos while their mother is out. He wrote it using only a few hundred simple words to help kids learn to read."],
  ["what is where the wild things are about", "***Where the Wild Things Are*** (1963) by **Maurice Sendak** is about Max, a boy sent to bed without supper after making mischief in his wolf suit. His room turns into a forest, and he sails to the land of the Wild Things, where he becomes their king. When he gets lonely, he sails home to find his supper waiting, still hot."],
  ["what is the very hungry caterpillar about", "***The Very Hungry Caterpillar*** (1969) by **Eric Carle** follows a tiny caterpillar who eats through more and more food each day of the week, with holes in the pages where he chomps through. After a stomachache and a nap in a cocoon, he becomes a beautiful butterfly. It teaches counting, days of the week and the life cycle."],
  ["what is cyrano de bergerac about", "***Cyrano de Bergerac*** (1897) is a French play by **Edmond Rostand**. Cyrano is a brilliant poet and swordsman with a very large nose who secretly loves his cousin Roxane, but believes she could never love him. He helps the handsome but tongue-tied Christian win her heart by writing his love letters for him."],
  ["what is the importance of being earnest about", "***The Importance of Being Earnest*** (1895) is a comic play by **Oscar Wilde**. Two young gentlemen both pretend to be named \"Ernest\" to impress the women they love, leading to a tangle of mistaken identities and very witty lines. It pokes fun at the manners of upper-class Victorian society."],
  ["what is the raven poem", "**Edgar Allan Poe** (1809–1849) was an American writer famous for spooky stories and poems and for inventing the detective story. In his poem ***The Raven***, a grieving man is visited at midnight by a mysterious raven that answers every question with one word: \"Nevermore.\""],
  ["what is the road not taken about", "**Robert Frost** (1874–1963) was an American poet who wrote about New England's countryside in plain but deep language. In ***The Road Not Taken***, a traveler chooses between two paths in a yellow wood and reflects on how choices shape our lives. His poem \"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening\" is also much loved."],
  ["what is a ballad | ballad poem meaning", "A **ballad** is a poem or song that tells a story, usually in short four-line stanzas with a simple rhyme scheme and often a repeated refrain. Ballads were passed down orally for centuries, telling tales of love, adventure and legend, like the old ballads of Robin Hood."],
  ["what is an ode | ode poem meaning", "An **ode** is a poem that praises or celebrates a person, object, idea or event, often in an elevated, emotional style. John Keats's \"Ode to a Nightingale\" is famous, and Pablo Neruda even wrote cheerful odes to everyday things like socks and tomatoes."],
  ["what is an elegy poem", "An **elegy** is a thoughtful poem written to mourn someone who has died or to reflect on loss. Walt Whitman's \"O Captain! My Captain!\", written after Abraham Lincoln's death, is a famous elegy."],
  ["what is an epic poem | examples of epic poems", "An **epic** is a very long narrative poem about the great deeds of heroes, often involving gods, journeys and battles. Famous epics include the *Epic of Gilgamesh*, Homer's *Iliad* and *Odyssey*, Virgil's *Aeneid*, *Beowulf* and India's *Ramayana*."],
  ["what is a villanelle", "A **villanelle** is a 19-line poem with five three-line stanzas and a final four-line stanza. It uses only two rhyme sounds, and two lines repeat over and over as refrains, which gives it a haunting, circular feel. Dylan Thomas's \"Do not go gentle into that good night\" is a famous one."],
  ["what is a ghazal | what is a tanka poem", "A **ghazal** is a poetic form from Arabic and Persian poetry made of couplets, each ending with the same repeated word or phrase, and traditionally about love and longing. A **tanka** is a Japanese poem of five lines with a 5-7-5-7-7 syllable pattern, like a haiku with two extra lines."],
  ["what is a sestina | what is blank verse", "A **sestina** is a complex 39-line poem in which the same six end-words repeat in a shifting pattern in each of six stanzas, then all appear in a final three-line stanza. **Blank verse** is poetry with a regular rhythm, usually iambic pentameter, but no rhyme, which Shakespeare used in much of his plays."],
  ["what is a couplet | what is a quatrain | what is a tercet", "A **couplet** is two lines of poetry together, often rhyming. A **tercet** is a group of three lines, and a **quatrain** is a stanza of four lines, the most common stanza in English poetry."],
  ["what is a concrete poem | what is a shape poem", "A **concrete poem** (or shape poem) arranges its words into a picture of the subject, like a poem about a tree written in the shape of a tree, or a poem about rain falling down the page. How the poem looks is part of its meaning."],
  ["what is a diamante poem | how to write a diamante", "A **diamante** is a seven-line poem shaped like a diamond. Line 1: one noun; line 2: two adjectives; line 3: three \"-ing\" verbs; line 4: four nouns; line 5: three \"-ing\" verbs; line 6: two adjectives; line 7: one noun. It often moves from one idea to its opposite, like day to night."],
  ["what is a narrative poem | what is a lyric poem", "A **narrative poem** tells a story with characters and a plot, like a ballad or an epic. A **lyric poem** is usually shorter and expresses the speaker's personal feelings or thoughts, like many sonnets and odes."],
  ["what is a clerihew | what is a nursery rhyme", "A **clerihew** is a funny four-line poem about a person whose name appears in the first line, with two rhyming couplets. A **nursery rhyme** is a short traditional poem or song for young children, like \"Humpty Dumpty\" or \"Hey Diddle Diddle,\" often with catchy rhythms and rhymes."],
  ["what is spoken word poetry | what is slam poetry", "**Spoken word** poetry is written to be performed out loud, using rhythm, voice and gesture. A **poetry slam** is a competition where poets perform their work, often in a few minutes, and audience members or judges score them."],
  ["what is enjambment | what is a refrain in poetry", "**Enjambment** is when a sentence in a poem runs past the end of a line without a pause, flowing onto the next line. A **refrain** is a line or group of lines that repeats throughout a poem or song, like a chorus."],
  ["who was charles perrault", "**Charles Perrault** (1628–1703) was a French writer who published a famous book of fairy tales in 1697, sometimes called *Tales of Mother Goose*. It included early written versions of \"Cinderella,\" \"Sleeping Beauty,\" \"Little Red Riding Hood\" and \"Puss in Boots.\" He's often called the father of the fairy-tale genre."],
  ["what are aesop's fables", "**Aesop** is said to have been a storyteller in ancient Greece around 600 BC, possibly once enslaved. **Aesop's Fables** are short stories, usually starring animals who act like people, that each end with a lesson or moral, like \"slow and steady wins the race.\" Many of the fables were probably collected from different storytellers over time."],
  ["what is the story of cinderella | cinderella story", "**Cinderella** is a kind young woman treated like a servant by her stepmother and stepsisters. With help from her fairy godmother, who turns a pumpkin into a coach, she goes to the royal ball, where the prince falls in love with her. She must leave by midnight and loses a glass slipper, which the prince uses to find her. Versions of this tale exist in cultures all over the world."],
  ["what is the story of snow white | snow white story", "**Snow White** is a princess whose vain stepmother, the queen, is jealous because her magic mirror says Snow White is \"the fairest of them all.\" Snow White escapes into the forest and lives with seven dwarfs. The queen tricks her with a poisoned apple, sending her into a deep sleep, but she's revived and lives happily ever after."],
  ["what is the story of sleeping beauty | sleeping beauty story", "In **Sleeping Beauty**, an angry fairy curses a baby princess to prick her finger on a spinning wheel's spindle. Another fairy softens the curse so that, instead of the worst, the princess falls asleep for 100 years. A thorny hedge grows around the castle until a prince arrives and the spell is broken."],
  ["what is the story of little red riding hood", "**Little Red Riding Hood** sets off through the woods to bring food to her grandmother. A sly wolf learns where she's going, races ahead and disguises himself as Grandma. \"What big eyes you have!\" she says. In the Grimm version, a woodcutter or hunter rescues them both. The lesson: don't talk to strangers or stray from the path."],
  ["what is the story of hansel and gretel", "**Hansel and Gretel** are siblings who get lost in the forest. Hansel first leaves a trail of pebbles, but later the birds eat his breadcrumb trail. The children find a house made of gingerbread and candy, owned by a wicked witch who traps them. Clever Gretel tricks the witch, and the children escape home with treasure."],
  ["what is the story of rapunzel | rapunzel let down your hair", "**Rapunzel** is a girl with extremely long golden hair, locked in a tall tower with no door by a witch. The witch climbs up by calling \"Rapunzel, Rapunzel, let down your hair!\" A prince hears her singing and climbs up the same way. After many troubles, the two are reunited and live happily."],
  ["what is the story of rumpelstiltskin", "In **Rumpelstiltskin**, a miller boasts that his daughter can spin straw into gold, so the king locks her up to do it. A strange little man spins the gold for her in exchange for her necklace, her ring and finally a promise of her firstborn child. He agrees to let her off if she can guess his name, and she does: Rumpelstiltskin!"],
  ["what is the story of jack and the beanstalk", "**Jack** trades his family's cow for a handful of magic beans. His mother throws them out the window, and overnight they grow into a gigantic beanstalk reaching the clouds. Jack climbs up to a giant's castle (\"Fee-fi-fo-fum!\"), takes a hen that lays golden eggs and a magic harp, then escapes down the beanstalk and chops it down."],
  ["what is the story of goldilocks and the three bears", "**Goldilocks** wanders into the empty house of three bears. She tries their porridge, chairs and beds: one too hot, one too cold and one \"just right.\" When the bears come home and find her asleep in Baby Bear's bed, she wakes up and runs away. Today, \"Goldilocks zone\" means something that's just right, like a planet that's neither too hot nor too cold."],
  ["what is the story of the three little pigs", "Three little pigs each build a house: one of straw, one of sticks and one of bricks. A big bad wolf huffs and puffs and blows down the straw and stick houses, but he can't blow down the brick house. The lesson is that hard work and careful planning pay off."],
  ["what is the story of the gingerbread man", "A woman bakes a **gingerbread man** who jumps out of the oven and runs away, shouting, \"Run, run, as fast as you can! You can't catch me, I'm the Gingerbread Man!\" He outruns everyone until he meets a clever fox who offers to carry him across a river, and the fox gobbles him up. The story warns against being too boastful."],
  ["what is the story of the little mermaid", "In Hans Christian Andersen's **The Little Mermaid** (1837), a young mermaid falls in love with a human prince. She trades her beautiful voice to a sea witch for human legs. Andersen's original has a bittersweet ending in which the mermaid's kindness earns her a new kind of life, while the Disney film gave it a happy ending."],
  ["what is the story of the ugly duckling", "In **The Ugly Duckling** by Hans Christian Andersen, a little bird hatches looking different from his siblings and is teased by all the farmyard animals. He spends a lonely winter, but in spring he sees his reflection and discovers he has grown into a beautiful **swan**. It's a story about growing into who you are."],
  ["what is the story of the emperor's new clothes", "In **The Emperor's New Clothes** by Hans Christian Andersen, two swindlers promise a vain emperor a magnificent outfit that's invisible to anyone foolish. Nobody wants to look foolish, so everyone praises the \"clothes,\" until a child in the parade shouts, \"But he isn't wearing anything!\" It shows the courage of telling the truth."],
  ["what is the story of the princess and the pea", "In **The Princess and the Pea** by Hans Christian Andersen, a prince wants to marry a real princess. A soaking-wet girl arrives at the castle on a stormy night, so the queen tests her by placing a single pea under 20 mattresses. In the morning the girl says she slept terribly because something hard was in the bed, proving she's a true princess."],
  ["what is the story of the snow queen | what is thumbelina about", "In **The Snow Queen**, a boy named Kai gets a splinter of a magic mirror in his eye, which makes him cold-hearted, and the Snow Queen carries him away. His brave friend Gerda journeys to rescue him, and her warm tears melt the splinter. **Thumbelina** is a girl no bigger than a thumb who has many adventures before finding a home among the flower fairies. Both are by Hans Christian Andersen."],
  ["what is the story of the frog prince", "In **The Frog Prince** by the Brothers Grimm, a princess drops her golden ball into a well, and a frog fetches it after she promises to be his friend. She tries to break her promise, but the king makes her keep it. The frog turns out to be an enchanted prince, and the spell breaks."],
  ["what is the story of beauty and the beast", "In **Beauty and the Beast**, a merchant picks a rose from a beast's garden, and his kind daughter Belle (Beauty) goes to live in the Beast's castle in his place. Over time she sees past his frightening looks to his gentle heart. Her love breaks the spell, turning him back into a prince. The best-known version was written in France in 1756."],
  ["what is the story of puss in boots", "In **Puss in Boots**, a poor young man inherits only a cat, but this cat is clever and asks for a pair of boots. Through cunning tricks, including fooling an ogre into turning himself into a mouse, Puss wins his master a castle, a fortune and a marriage to a princess."],
  ["what is the story of the elves and the shoemaker", "A poor shoemaker has leather for only one last pair of shoes. Each night, while he sleeps, tiny **elves** secretly make beautiful shoes, and he becomes successful. To thank them, he and his wife sew the elves little clothes, and the delighted elves dance away. It's a story about kindness and gratitude."],
  ["what is the story of the little red hen", "The **Little Red Hen** finds some wheat and asks her friends, the cat, dog and duck, to help plant, cut, grind and bake it. Each time they say, \"Not I!\" When the bread is ready, they all want to eat it, but she eats it herself. The lesson: those who don't help with the work shouldn't expect to share the reward."],
  ["what is the story of the three billy goats gruff", "Three billy goats named **Gruff** want to cross a bridge to eat green grass, but a grumpy troll lives under it. The smallest and middle goats each tell the troll to wait for their bigger brother. When the biggest goat arrives, he knocks the troll off the bridge with his horns, and the goats enjoy the meadow."],
  ["what is the story of chicken little | what is henny penny about", "In **Chicken Little** (or **Henny Penny**), an acorn falls on a chick's head and she panics, crying \"The sky is falling!\" She gathers other animals to warn the king, but a sly fox tricks them. The story teaches not to panic or believe everything you hear without checking."],
  ["what is the story of the tortoise and the hare", "In Aesop's fable, a boastful **hare** challenges a slow **tortoise** to a race. The hare zooms ahead and takes a nap, sure he'll win. The tortoise keeps plodding along without stopping and crosses the finish line first. Moral: **slow and steady wins the race**."],
  ["what is the story of the boy who cried wolf", "In this Aesop fable, a bored shepherd boy shouts \"Wolf!\" to trick the villagers into running up the hill, then laughs at them. He does it again. When a real wolf finally comes and he cries for help, nobody believes him. Moral: **liars aren't believed even when they tell the truth**."],
  ["what is the fox and the grapes about | what does sour grapes mean", "In Aesop's fable, a fox sees juicy grapes hanging high on a vine but can't reach them no matter how he jumps. He walks away saying, \"They were probably sour anyway.\" That's where the phrase **\"sour grapes\"** comes from: pretending you don't want something you couldn't get."],
  ["what is the ant and the grasshopper about", "In Aesop's fable, all summer the **ant** works hard storing food while the **grasshopper** sings and plays. When winter comes, the grasshopper is hungry and has nothing, while the ant is ready. Moral: **work hard and prepare for the future**."],
  ["what is the lion and the mouse about", "In Aesop's fable, a lion catches a tiny mouse but lets it go when the mouse promises to repay him someday. The lion laughs, but later he's caught in a hunter's net, and the mouse gnaws through the ropes to free him. Moral: **no act of kindness is ever wasted**, and even the small can help the mighty."],
  ["what is the crow and the pitcher about", "In Aesop's fable, a thirsty crow finds a pitcher with a little water at the bottom, too low for its beak to reach. It drops pebbles in one by one until the water rises high enough to drink. Moral: **cleverness and persistence solve problems**. Scientists have found real crows can solve this very puzzle!"],
  ["what is the goose that laid the golden eggs about", "In Aesop's fable, a farmer owns a goose that lays one golden egg each day. Growing greedy and impatient, he thinks there must be a pile of gold inside her, so he harms the goose, and finds nothing, losing his daily golden egg forever. Moral: **greed can make you lose what you already have**."],
  ["what is the town mouse and the country mouse about", "In Aesop's fable, a town mouse visits his cousin in the country and sneers at the simple food. Then the country mouse visits the city, where the food is fancy but they're constantly chased by cats and dogs. The country mouse goes home, saying he'd rather eat plain food in peace. Moral: **a simple, safe life beats a rich, fearful one**."],
  ["what is the north wind and the sun about", "In Aesop's fable, the **North Wind** and the **Sun** argue about who's stronger and test it by trying to make a traveler take off his cloak. The harder the Wind blows, the tighter the man holds his cloak. Then the Sun shines warmly, and the man takes it off. Moral: **gentleness and kindness often work better than force**."],
  ["what is the fox and the crow about", "In Aesop's fable, a crow sits in a tree with a piece of cheese. A fox flatters her, saying her voice must be as beautiful as her feathers. When the crow opens her beak to sing, the cheese falls and the fox snatches it. Moral: **beware of flatterers**."],
  ["what is the dog and his reflection about", "In Aesop's fable, a dog carrying a bone crosses a bridge and sees his reflection in the water. Thinking it's another dog with a bigger bone, he snaps at it and drops his own bone into the stream. Moral: **greed can make you lose what you have**."],
  ["what is the wolf in sheep's clothing about", "In this fable, a wolf disguises himself in a sheep's skin to sneak into the flock. The phrase **\"a wolf in sheep's clothing\"** now describes someone who pretends to be friendly or harmless but has bad intentions. Moral: **appearances can be deceiving**."],
  ["what is belling the cat about | who will bell the cat", "In this fable, mice meet to decide how to protect themselves from a cat. A young mouse suggests hanging a bell on the cat's neck so they'll hear it coming, and everyone cheers, until an old mouse asks, \"But who will bell the cat?\" Moral: **an idea is easy to suggest but hard to carry out**."],
  ["what is the story of the pied piper of hamelin", "In the legend of the **Pied Piper of Hamelin**, a German town is overrun by rats. A piper in colorful clothes plays his magical pipe, and the rats follow him into the river. When the town refuses to pay him, he plays again, and the children follow him away. The lesson: **keep your promises**."],
  ["what is the story of aladdin", "**Aladdin** is a poor young man tricked by a sorcerer into fetching a magic lamp from a cave. When he rubs the lamp, a powerful **genie** appears who grants his wishes. With the genie's help, he becomes rich, marries a princess and outsmarts the sorcerer. The tale was added to *One Thousand and One Nights* in the 1700s."],
  ["what is the story of ali baba | open sesame meaning", "**Ali Baba** is a poor woodcutter who overhears a band of forty thieves open their treasure cave with the magic words **\"Open, Sesame!\"** He takes some gold, and the thieves come after him, but his clever servant Morgiana outwits them. It's one of the famous tales associated with *The Arabian Nights*."],
  ["who was sinbad the sailor", "**Sinbad the Sailor** is a hero of tales associated with *One Thousand and One Nights*. On seven voyages he's shipwrecked again and again, meeting a giant bird called the **roc**, a whale so big sailors mistake it for an island and a valley of diamonds, always escaping by luck and cleverness."],
  ["who is anansi | anansi the spider stories", "**Anansi** is a clever trickster spider from the folk tales of the Akan people of Ghana in West Africa. Stories about him spread to the Caribbean and the Americas. In one famous tale, Anansi wins all the world's stories from the sky god, which is why stories are sometimes called \"spider stories.\""],
  ["who was paul bunyan | who was john henry", "**Paul Bunyan** is a giant lumberjack from American tall tales, so big his footprints made lakes, with a huge blue ox named Babe. **John Henry** is an African American folk hero, a mighty steel-driving railroad worker who, legend says, raced against a steam-powered drill and won."],
  ["robin hood legend", "**Robin Hood** is a legendary English outlaw who lived in **Sherwood Forest** with his band of Merry Men, including Little John and Friar Tuck. He was a brilliant archer who \"robbed from the rich and gave to the poor,\" opposing the unfair Sheriff of Nottingham. Ballads about him go back to the 1400s."],
  ["what is the legend of king arthur", "**King Arthur** is a legendary British king. In the stories, young Arthur proves he's the rightful king by pulling a sword from a stone. He rules from the castle of **Camelot** with Queen Guinevere, advised by the wizard **Merlin**, and leads the Knights of the Round Table on noble quests. Historians debate whether a real Arthur ever existed."],
  ["what is excalibur | what is the sword in the stone", "In the Arthurian legends, the **sword in the stone** could only be pulled out by the true king of Britain, and young Arthur did it. **Excalibur** is Arthur's magical sword; in many versions it's given to him by the Lady of the Lake. Some tales say they're the same sword, others that they're different."],
  ["what are the knights of the round table | who was sir lancelot", "The **Knights of the Round Table** were King Arthur's bravest knights. The table was round so no knight sat at the head and all were equal. Famous knights include **Sir Lancelot**, the greatest warrior, **Sir Galahad**, who found the Holy Grail, and **Sir Gawain**."],
  ["who was merlin | merlin the wizard", "**Merlin** is the wise wizard and prophet of the King Arthur legends. He helps arrange Arthur's birth, guides him as a young king and uses magic and prophecy to protect Camelot. In some stories he lives backward in time, which is why he knows the future."],
  ["what is the legend of sleepy hollow | who is the headless horseman", "***The Legend of Sleepy Hollow*** (1820) by **Washington Irving** is set in a sleepy village in New York. A nervous schoolteacher, **Ichabod Crane**, competes for a young woman's attention and then, riding home late one night, is chased by the legendary **Headless Horseman**. He's never seen in the village again, and readers are left wondering what really happened."],
  ["what is the story of rip van winkle", "In Washington Irving's tale, **Rip Van Winkle** is a friendly but lazy man in a village in the Catskill Mountains. He wanders into the hills, drinks with strange little men and falls asleep, waking up 20 years later with a long white beard, to find the American Revolution has happened while he slept."],
  ["what is the story of momotaro | who is peach boy", "**Momotarō** (\"Peach Boy\") is a Japanese folk tale. An old couple find a giant peach floating in a river, and inside is a baby boy. When he grows up, he sets off to defeat the ogres (oni) on Ogre Island, making friends with a dog, a monkey and a pheasant by sharing his millet dumplings, and returns home with treasure."],
  ["who is zeus | tell me about zeus | zeus greek god", "**Zeus** was the king of the Greek gods, ruling from Mount Olympus. He was the god of the sky and thunder, often shown with a lightning bolt and an eagle. The Romans called him **Jupiter**."],
  ["who is hera | hera greek goddess", "**Hera** was the queen of the Greek gods and the wife of Zeus. She was the goddess of marriage, women and families, and her sacred animals were the peacock and the cow. She was known for her jealousy toward Zeus's many romances. The Romans called her **Juno**."],
  ["who is poseidon | poseidon god of the sea", "**Poseidon** was the Greek god of the sea, earthquakes and horses, and a brother of Zeus. He carried a three-pronged spear called a **trident** and could stir up storms or calm the waves. The Romans called him **Neptune**."],
  ["who is hades | hades god of the underworld", "**Hades** was the Greek god of the underworld, the realm where the ancient Greeks believed souls went after life. He was a brother of Zeus and Poseidon and ruled with his queen, Persephone. His three-headed dog, **Cerberus**, guarded the gates. He also ruled over riches from the earth, like gold and gems. The Romans called him **Pluto**."],
  ["who is athena | athena goddess of wisdom", "**Athena** was the Greek goddess of wisdom, crafts and strategy in war. In myth she sprang from Zeus's head fully grown and wearing armor! Her symbol is the **owl**, and the city of Athens was named after her, with the Parthenon built in her honor. The Romans called her **Minerva**."],
  ["who is apollo | apollo greek god", "**Apollo** was the Greek god of music, poetry, light, healing and prophecy. He played a golden lyre and was linked with the sun. His most famous temple was at **Delphi**, where his priestess, the Oracle, gave prophecies. He was the twin brother of Artemis, and the Romans also called him Apollo."],
  ["who is artemis | artemis goddess of the hunt", "**Artemis** was the Greek goddess of the hunt, wild animals, the wilderness and the moon. She carried a silver bow and arrows and protected young girls and animals. She was Apollo's twin sister. The Romans called her **Diana**."],
  ["who is ares | ares god of war", "**Ares** was the Greek god of war, representing its fierce and violent side, while Athena represented its strategy. He was the son of Zeus and Hera, and his sacred animals included the dog and the vulture. The Romans called him **Mars**, and they honored Mars much more than the Greeks honored Ares."],
  ["who is aphrodite | aphrodite goddess of love", "**Aphrodite** was the Greek goddess of love and beauty. One myth says she was born from the sea foam and came ashore on a scallop shell, as shown in Botticelli's *The Birth of Venus*. Her symbols include the dove and the rose. The Romans called her **Venus**."],
  ["who is hephaestus | hephaestus god of fire", "**Hephaestus** was the Greek god of fire, blacksmiths and craftsmanship. He worked at a forge, sometimes said to be under a volcano, making weapons and armor for the gods, including Zeus's thunderbolts and Achilles' shield. He walked with a limp, but was the most skilled craftsman on Olympus. The Romans called him **Vulcan**."],
  ["who is hermes | hermes messenger god", "**Hermes** was the messenger of the Greek gods, zooming around in winged sandals and a winged hat. He was the god of travelers, merchants, thieves and trickery, and he guided souls to the underworld. As a baby he invented the lyre and stole Apollo's cattle! The Romans called him **Mercury**."],
  ["who is demeter | demeter goddess of harvest", "**Demeter** was the Greek goddess of the harvest, grain and farming. Her grief when her daughter Persephone went to the underworld was said to cause winter. The Romans called her **Ceres**, which is where the word \"cereal\" comes from."],
  ["who is dionysus | dionysus greek god", "**Dionysus** was the Greek god of grapes and wine-making, festivals, celebrations and theater. Ancient Greek plays were first performed at festivals in his honor, so he's linked to the birth of drama. He was often shown with grapevines and ivy. The Romans called him **Bacchus**."],
  ["who is hestia | hestia goddess of the hearth", "**Hestia** was the Greek goddess of the hearth, home and family. She was the oldest child of Cronus and Rhea and was known for being calm and kind. A fire in her honor was kept burning in homes and town halls. The Romans called her **Vesta**."],
  ["who is persephone | persephone and the seasons", "**Persephone** was the daughter of Demeter. Hades carried her off to the underworld to be his queen, and Demeter's sadness made all the crops stop growing. Because Persephone had eaten a few pomegranate seeds there, she had to spend part of each year in the underworld. The Greeks said that's why we have winter, and spring returns when she comes back."],
  ["who is eros | who is cupid", "**Eros** was the Greek god of love, often shown as a winged youth with a bow and arrows that made people fall in love. The Romans called him **Cupid** and pictured him as a chubby winged child, which is why he appears on Valentine's Day cards."],
  ["who is nike the goddess | nike goddess of victory", "**Nike** was the Greek goddess of victory, shown with wings, often holding a wreath. A famous ancient statue of her, the *Winged Victory of Samothrace*, stands in the Louvre. The sportswear brand is named after her. The Romans called her **Victoria**."],
  ["who is gaia | who is uranus in greek mythology", "In Greek myth, **Gaia** was Mother Earth, one of the first beings to appear at the beginning of creation. She and **Uranus**, the sky, were the parents of the Titans, the Cyclopes and other powerful beings. Uranus is also the only planet named after a Greek god rather than a Roman one."],
  ["who are the titans | who is cronus", "The **Titans** were a race of powerful gods who ruled before the Olympians. Their leader was **Cronus** (Kronos), who feared his children would overthrow him. His youngest son, Zeus, escaped, then rescued his siblings, and they defeated the Titans in a great war called the Titanomachy."],
  ["who is prometheus | why did prometheus steal fire", "**Prometheus** was a Titan who loved humanity. In myth he stole fire from the gods and gave it to people, helping them cook, stay warm and build civilization. Zeus punished him by chaining him to a mountain, until the hero Heracles eventually set him free."],
  ["who is atlas | why does atlas hold up the sky", "**Atlas** was a Titan who fought against Zeus in the war of the Titans. As punishment he was made to hold up the sky on his shoulders forever. A book of maps is called an \"atlas\" because early map books showed him on the cover, and the Atlantic Ocean is named after him."],
  ["who is helios | who is selene | who is eos", "In Greek myth, **Helios** drove the chariot of the sun across the sky each day, his sister **Selene** was the goddess of the moon, and their sister **Eos** was the goddess of the dawn, opening the gates of heaven each morning. The Romans called them Sol, Luna and Aurora."],
  ["who is pan the god | pan greek god", "**Pan** was the Greek god of the wild, shepherds and flocks. He had the legs and horns of a goat and played the panpipes, made of reeds of different lengths. The word \"panic\" comes from the sudden fear he was said to cause in lonely places."],
  ["who are the muses | nine muses", "The **nine Muses** were Greek goddesses of the arts and knowledge, daughters of Zeus and Mnemosyne (Memory). Each had her own area, such as Calliope for epic poetry, Clio for history, Terpsichore for dance and Urania for astronomy. The words \"music\" and \"museum\" come from them."],
  ["who are the fates | the three fates greek mythology", "The **three Fates** (Moirai) were Greek goddesses who decided the destiny of every person. **Clotho** spun the thread of life, **Lachesis** measured it and **Atropos** cut it. Even the gods were said to respect their decisions."],
  ["what are nymphs in greek mythology | what is a dryad", "**Nymphs** were nature spirits in Greek myth, usually shown as young women living in natural places. **Dryads** lived in trees, **naiads** in rivers, springs and lakes, **nereids** in the sea and **oreads** in the mountains."],
  ["who are the 12 olympians | list the greek gods", "The **Twelve Olympians** were the main Greek gods who lived on Mount Olympus: **Zeus, Hera, Poseidon, Demeter, Athena, Apollo, Artemis, Ares, Aphrodite, Hephaestus, Hermes** and either **Hestia** or **Dionysus**. Some lists say Hestia gave up her seat to Dionysus. Hades is usually not counted because he lived in the underworld."],
  ["what are the roman names of greek gods | greek and roman gods list", "Greek → Roman: **Zeus → Jupiter**, **Hera → Juno**, **Poseidon → Neptune**, **Hades → Pluto**, **Athena → Minerva**, **Ares → Mars**, **Aphrodite → Venus**, **Artemis → Diana**, **Apollo → Apollo**, **Hermes → Mercury**, **Hephaestus → Vulcan**, **Demeter → Ceres**, **Dionysus → Bacchus**, **Hestia → Vesta** and **Eros → Cupid**."],
  ["who is jupiter the god | jupiter roman god", "**Jupiter** was the king of the Roman gods and god of the sky and thunder, matching the Greek Zeus. The Romans built a great temple to him on Rome's Capitoline Hill. The biggest planet is named after him, fittingly for the king of the gods."],
  ["who is mars the god | mars roman god of war", "**Mars** was the Roman god of war and one of Rome's most important gods, said to be the father of Romulus and Remus, the founders of Rome. He was also linked with farming and spring. The red planet and the month of **March** are named after him."],
  ["who is janus | why is january named after janus", "**Janus** was a Roman god of beginnings, doorways and transitions, shown with **two faces**, one looking to the past and one to the future. He had no Greek equivalent. The month of **January**, the start of a new year, is named after him."],
  ["who were romulus and remus", "According to Roman legend, **Romulus and Remus** were twin brothers who were set adrift on the Tiber River as babies. A she-wolf found and cared for them, and later a shepherd raised them. As adults they decided to found a city, but they quarreled, and Romulus became its founder and first king, naming it **Rome** after himself, traditionally in 753 BC."],
  ["why are planets named after roman gods | which planets are named after gods", "The ancient Romans named the planets they could see after their gods, and we still use those names. **Mercury** moves fastest, like the messenger god; **Venus** is the brightest and most beautiful; **Mars** is red like blood and war; **Jupiter** is the biggest, like the king; **Saturn** is named after Jupiter's father. **Neptune** (blue, like the sea) and **Uranus** were named later."],
  ["who was hercules | who was heracles", "**Heracles** (called **Hercules** by the Romans) was the greatest hero of Greek myth, the son of Zeus and a mortal woman, and famous for his incredible strength. Even as a baby he wrestled two snakes sent to his cradle! He's best known for completing the Twelve Labors, and after his life on Earth he was welcomed to Olympus as a god."],
  ["what are the 12 labors of hercules | twelve labours of heracles", "The **Twelve Labors** were tasks given to Heracles: 1) defeat the Nemean Lion, 2) the many-headed Hydra, 3) catch the Golden Hind of Artemis, 4) the Erymanthian Boar, 5) clean the Augean stables in one day, 6) drive off the Stymphalian Birds, 7) the Cretan Bull, 8) the Mares of Diomedes, 9) get the belt of Hippolyta, queen of the Amazons, 10) the cattle of Geryon, 11) the golden apples of the Hesperides and 12) bring back Cerberus from the underworld."],
  ["how did hercules clean the augean stables", "King Augeas had thousands of cattle, and his stables hadn't been cleaned in 30 years! Heracles had to clean them in a single day. He cleverly dug channels and changed the course of two rivers so they rushed through the stables and washed everything away."],
  ["what is the hydra | how did hercules defeat the hydra", "The **Hydra** was a giant serpent-like monster with many heads, living in a swamp at Lerna. Every time Heracles cut off one head, two more grew back! His nephew Iolaus helped by sealing each neck with a burning torch so no new heads could grow, and together they defeated it."],
  ["who was perseus | how did perseus defeat medusa", "**Perseus** was a Greek hero, son of Zeus. Sent to fetch the head of **Medusa**, whose gaze turned people to stone, he used gifts from the gods: winged sandals, a helmet of invisibility and a shiny shield. By looking only at Medusa's reflection in the shield, he could defeat her safely. On his way home he rescued Princess Andromeda from a sea monster."],
  ["who is medusa | why does medusa have snakes for hair", "**Medusa** was one of the three Gorgons in Greek myth, with snakes for hair and a gaze that turned anyone who looked at her into stone. In one version she was once a beautiful woman cursed by Athena. The hero Perseus defeated her by using a mirrored shield, and the winged horse **Pegasus** sprang from her."],
  ["who was theseus | what is the minotaur", "**Theseus** was a hero of Athens. The **Minotaur**, a creature half man and half bull, lived in the **Labyrinth**, a giant maze on Crete built by Daedalus. Princess Ariadne gave Theseus a ball of thread to unwind as he went in, so after defeating the Minotaur, he could follow the thread back out."],
  ["who was jason | what is the golden fleece | who were the argonauts", "**Jason** was a Greek hero who sailed on the ship **Argo** with a crew of heroes called the **Argonauts**, including Heracles and Orpheus. Their quest was to bring back the **Golden Fleece**, the wool of a magical golden ram, guarded by a dragon that never slept. With help from the sorceress Medea, Jason succeeded."],
  ["who was achilles | what is an achilles heel", "**Achilles** was the greatest Greek warrior of the Trojan War. Legend says his mother dipped him in the River Styx as a baby to make him invulnerable, but she held him by the heel, so that one spot stayed unprotected. That's why an **\"Achilles' heel\"** means a person's one weak spot, and the tendon at the back of your ankle is called the Achilles tendon."],
  ["who was odysseus | who is ulysses", "**Odysseus** (called **Ulysses** by the Romans) was the king of Ithaca and the cleverest of the Greek heroes. He came up with the idea of the Trojan Horse, and his ten-year journey home after the war is told in Homer's *Odyssey*. He was famous for using his wits rather than strength."],
  ["what was the trojan war | why did the trojan war start", "The **Trojan War** was a legendary ten-year war between the Greeks and the city of **Troy**. Myth says it began when Paris, a prince of Troy, took **Helen**, the queen of Sparta, said to be the most beautiful woman in the world, back to Troy with him. Her husband Menelaus and his brother Agamemnon led a huge Greek army to get her back. Archaeologists believe a real city of Troy existed in what's now Turkey."],
  ["what is the trojan horse | how did the trojan horse work", "After ten years of failing to capture Troy, the Greeks built a giant wooden horse, hid soldiers inside and pretended to sail away. The Trojans dragged the horse into their city as a victory trophy. That night the hidden soldiers crept out and opened the gates for the Greek army. Today a \"Trojan horse\" means a trick that hides danger inside a gift, including a type of computer malware."],
  ["who was helen of troy | the face that launched a thousand ships", "**Helen of Troy** was said to be the most beautiful woman in the world, a daughter of Zeus and queen of Sparta. When she went to Troy with Prince Paris, the Greeks launched a great fleet to bring her back, starting the Trojan War. That's why she's called \"the face that launched a thousand ships.\""],
  ["what is the cyclops story | how did odysseus escape the cyclops", "In the *Odyssey*, Odysseus and his men are trapped in the cave of **Polyphemus**, a one-eyed giant Cyclops. Odysseus tells him his name is **\"Nobody.\"** After Odysseus blinds the Cyclops' one eye with a sharpened stake, Polyphemus shouts that \"Nobody\" is hurting him, so the other Cyclopes don't come to help. The men escape by clinging to the bellies of his sheep."],
  ["who were the sirens | what is a siren in mythology", "The **Sirens** were creatures whose beautiful singing lured sailors to crash on their rocky island. To hear their song safely, Odysseus had his crew plug their ears with beeswax and tie him to the ship's mast. Today, an emergency **siren** is named after them."],
  ["what is scylla and charybdis | between a rock and a hard place", "In the *Odyssey*, ships had to pass between two dangers in a narrow strait: **Scylla**, a six-headed monster on a cliff, and **Charybdis**, a giant whirlpool. \"Between Scylla and Charybdis\" means being stuck between two bad choices, a lot like the phrase \"between a rock and a hard place.\""],
  ["what is pandora's box | what was in pandora's box", "**Pandora** was the first woman in Greek myth, created by the gods and given a jar (later called a \"box\") that she was told never to open. Curiosity got the better of her, and when she lifted the lid, all the world's troubles, like sickness, sorrow and hard work, flew out. She closed it just in time to keep **Hope** inside. \"Opening Pandora's box\" means starting something that causes lots of unexpected problems."],
  ["what is the story of icarus | icarus and daedalus", "**Daedalus**, a brilliant inventor, and his son **Icarus** were imprisoned on the island of Crete. Daedalus built wings of feathers and wax so they could fly away, warning Icarus not to fly too low near the sea or too high near the sun. Icarus, thrilled by flying, soared too high, the sun melted the wax, and he fell into the sea. The story warns about overconfidence."],
  ["what is the story of king midas | what is the midas touch", "**King Midas** was granted one wish by the god Dionysus, and he wished that everything he touched would turn to gold. At first he was delighted, but then his food and drink turned to gold too, and in some versions, so did his daughter when he hugged her. He begged to have the gift removed and washed it away in a river. Today, having \"the Midas touch\" means being good at making money."],
  ["what is the story of narcissus and echo", "**Echo** was a nymph cursed so she could only repeat the last words others said. She loved the handsome **Narcissus**, but he ignored everyone. Then Narcissus saw his own reflection in a pool and fell in love with it, staring until he turned into the narcissus flower. Echo faded away until only her voice remained. The word \"narcissist\" comes from him."],
  ["what is the story of arachne | why are spiders called arachnids", "**Arachne** was a mortal weaver so skilled that she boasted she was better than Athena, the goddess of weaving. Athena challenged her to a contest, and Arachne's tapestry was flawless but mocked the gods. Athena turned her into a spider so she could weave forever. That's why spiders are called **arachnids**."],
  ["what is the story of orpheus and eurydice", "**Orpheus** was a musician whose songs could charm animals, trees and even rocks. When his wife **Eurydice** died, he journeyed to the underworld and played so beautifully that Hades agreed to let her return, on one condition: Orpheus must not look back until they reached the surface. Just before the end he glanced back, and she slipped away. It's a story about love and trust."],
  ["what is the story of sisyphus | what does sisyphean mean", "**Sisyphus** was a clever but deceitful king who tricked the gods, even cheating death. As punishment, he had to roll a huge boulder up a hill forever, but every time he neared the top it rolled back down. A **Sisyphean** task is one that's endless and frustrating."],
  ["who was tantalus | where does the word tantalize come from", "**Tantalus** was a king who offended the gods. His punishment was to stand in a pool of water under a fruit tree, but whenever he bent to drink, the water drained away, and whenever he reached for fruit, the branches lifted out of reach. That's where the word **\"tantalize\"** comes from: to tease with something you can't quite have."],
  ["what is cerberus | the three headed dog", "**Cerberus** was the giant three-headed dog that guarded the gates of the underworld for Hades, making sure no one escaped. For his final labor, Heracles had to bring Cerberus up to the surface, and he did it with his bare hands. Orpheus once lulled Cerberus to sleep with music."],
  ["who rode pegasus", "**Pegasus** was a magnificent winged white horse in Greek myth, born when Perseus defeated Medusa. The hero **Bellerophon** tamed him with a golden bridle from Athena and rode him to defeat the **Chimera**, a fire-breathing monster with the parts of a lion, a goat and a serpent. Pegasus later became a constellation."],
  ["what is a centaur | who was chiron", "A **centaur** is a creature from Greek myth with the upper body of a human and the lower body of a horse. Most were wild, but **Chiron** was wise and kind, a skilled healer and teacher who trained heroes like Achilles, Jason and Asclepius."],
  ["what is the riddle of the sphinx", "The Greek **Sphinx**, with a lion's body, wings and a woman's head, guarded the city of Thebes and asked travelers a riddle: \"What walks on four legs in the morning, two legs at noon and three legs in the evening?\" The hero **Oedipus** answered: **a human**, who crawls as a baby, walks as an adult and uses a cane in old age."],
  ["what is a phoenix | phoenix mythology", "The **phoenix** is a legendary bird from ancient Greek and Egyptian stories that lives for hundreds of years. At the end of its life it bursts into flames and is reborn from its own ashes, young again. It's a symbol of renewal and of rising again after hard times."],
  ["what is mount olympus | is mount olympus real", "In Greek myth, **Mount Olympus** was the home of the twelve main gods, who lived in palaces above the clouds. It's also a real mountain, the highest in Greece at about 2,918 meters (9,573 feet), whose top is often hidden by clouds."],
  ["what is the underworld in greek mythology | what is the river styx", "In Greek myth, the **underworld** was the realm of Hades, where souls went after life. To get there, souls were ferried across the **River Styx** by the boatman **Charon**, who expected a coin as payment. The three-headed dog Cerberus guarded the entrance."],
  ["what is norse mythology | who are the norse gods", "**Norse mythology** is the collection of myths from the Vikings and other peoples of Scandinavia. Its gods include **Odin**, the wise all-father, **Thor**, god of thunder, the trickster **Loki**, **Frigg**, **Freya** and **Baldur**. Much of what we know comes from Icelandic texts written in the 1200s called the Eddas."],
  ["who is odin | odin norse god", "**Odin** was the chief of the Norse gods, the god of wisdom, war, poetry and magic. He gave up one of his eyes to drink from a well of wisdom, and he hung from the world tree for nine days to learn the secret of the runes. He had two ravens, Thought and Memory, who flew around the world and told him the news."],
  ["who is thor | thor norse god of thunder", "**Thor** was the Norse god of thunder, lightning and storms, and the protector of gods and humans. He was the son of Odin and was hugely strong, with a red beard and a mighty hammer. He rode across the sky in a chariot pulled by two goats, and Thursday is named after him (\"Thor's day\")."],
  ["what is mjolnir | what is thor's hammer", "**Mjölnir** was Thor's mighty hammer, made by dwarf blacksmiths. It could level mountains, always returned to his hand after he threw it and could shrink small enough to fit inside his shirt. Thor needed iron gloves and a belt of strength to wield it. Vikings often wore small hammer-shaped pendants for protection."],
  ["who is loki | loki norse trickster", "**Loki** was the trickster of Norse mythology, a shape-shifter who could become a salmon, a mare or a fly. Sometimes he helped the gods, getting them wonderful treasures like Thor's hammer, and sometimes he caused terrible trouble. In the myths, he plays a big role in bringing about Ragnarök."],
  ["who is frigg | who is freya", "**Frigg** was Odin's wife and the queen of the Norse gods, a goddess of marriage, motherhood and wisdom who could see the future. **Freya** was the goddess of love, beauty and magic, who rode in a chariot pulled by two cats and owned a cloak of falcon feathers. Friday may be named after either of them."],
  ["who is baldur | who is balder", "**Baldur** (Balder) was the Norse god of light, kindness and beauty, the much-loved son of Odin and Frigg. His mother made every thing in the world promise not to harm him, except the little mistletoe plant, which seemed too small to matter. Loki used that one exception to cause his death, which led toward Ragnarök."],
  ["who is tyr | who is heimdall", "**Tyr** was the Norse god of war, law and courage, who bravely gave up his hand so the gods could bind the giant wolf Fenrir. Tuesday is named after him. **Heimdall** was the watchman of the gods, guarding the rainbow bridge **Bifröst**; he could hear grass growing and would blow his horn, Gjallarhorn, to warn of Ragnarök."],
  ["what is yggdrasil | what is the world tree", "**Yggdrasil** is the giant ash tree at the center of the Norse universe, whose branches and roots connect the **nine worlds**. An eagle sits at its top, a dragon gnaws at its roots and a squirrel named Ratatoskr runs up and down carrying messages between them."],
  ["what are the nine realms | what are the nine worlds in norse mythology", "Norse myth describes **nine worlds** connected by Yggdrasil, including **Asgard** (home of the Aesir gods), **Midgard** (the human world), **Vanaheim** (the Vanir gods), **Jotunheim** (the giants), **Alfheim** (the light elves), **Svartalfheim** (the dwarves), **Niflheim** (a world of ice and mist), **Muspelheim** (a world of fire) and **Hel** (the realm of the dead)."],
  ["what is asgard | what is bifrost", "**Asgard** is the home of the Norse gods, with great halls like Odin's Valhalla. It's connected to Midgard, the world of humans, by **Bifröst**, a burning rainbow bridge guarded by the god Heimdall."],
  ["what is valhalla | who are the valkyries", "**Valhalla** is Odin's enormous golden hall in Asgard, with a roof made of shields. The **Valkyries** were warrior maidens who rode over battlefields choosing brave warriors to bring to Valhalla, where they feasted and trained together, preparing to help the gods at Ragnarök."],
  ["what is ragnarok | what happens in ragnarok", "**Ragnarök** is the great final battle in Norse mythology, a \"twilight of the gods.\" After three long winters, the giants and monsters fight the gods: Thor battles the World Serpent, and Odin faces the wolf Fenrir. The world is destroyed, but afterwards a new, green world rises from the sea and a few gods and two humans begin again."],
  ["who is fenrir | who is jormungandr", "**Fenrir** was a gigantic wolf, a child of Loki. The gods bound him with a magical ribbon made by dwarves from impossible things, like the sound of a cat's footsteps. **Jörmungandr**, the World Serpent, was another of Loki's children, so huge it encircles the whole human world and bites its own tail. Both play big roles at Ragnarök."],
  ["what is sleipnir | odin's eight legged horse", "**Sleipnir** was Odin's gray horse with **eight legs**, the fastest horse in the nine worlds, able to gallop through the air and over the sea. In the myths, Loki was its parent after turning himself into a mare."],
  ["why are the days of the week named after norse gods", "Several English weekday names come from Norse and Germanic gods: **Tuesday** is Tyr's day, **Wednesday** is Woden's (Odin's) day, **Thursday** is Thor's day and **Friday** is Frigg's or Freya's day. Saturday comes from the Roman god Saturn, and Sunday and Monday from the sun and moon."],
  ["what is egyptian mythology | who are the egyptian gods", "**Ancient Egyptian mythology** included hundreds of gods and goddesses, many shown with human bodies and animal heads. Important ones include **Ra**, the sun god, **Osiris**, ruler of the afterlife, **Isis**, goddess of magic and protection, **Horus**, the sky god, and **Anubis**, protector of tombs. They were worshipped for over 3,000 years."],
  ["who is ra | ra egyptian sun god", "**Ra** (or Re) was the ancient Egyptian sun god and one of the most important gods, often shown with a falcon's head topped by a sun disk. Egyptians believed he sailed across the sky in a boat each day, then traveled through the underworld at night, battling the chaos serpent Apophis, before rising again each morning."],
  ["who is osiris | osiris egyptian god", "**Osiris** was the Egyptian god of the afterlife, rebirth and the yearly flooding of the Nile that made crops grow. He is shown with green or black skin, symbols of fertile soil, wrapped like a mummy and wearing a tall white crown. In myth, his jealous brother Set defeated him, but his wife Isis brought him back, and he became ruler of the afterlife."],
  ["who is isis the goddess | isis egyptian goddess", "**Isis** was a beloved Egyptian goddess of magic, healing and motherhood, and the wife of Osiris and mother of Horus. She was famous for her loyalty and her powerful magic, which she used to restore Osiris. She's often shown wearing a throne-shaped headdress or cow horns with a sun disk."],
  ["who is horus | what is the eye of horus", "**Horus** was the Egyptian sky god with the head of a falcon, the son of Isis and Osiris. Pharaohs were seen as the living Horus. In myth he battled his uncle Set and lost an eye, which was magically healed. The **Eye of Horus** became a popular symbol of protection, healing and good health."],
  ["who is anubis | anubis egyptian god", "**Anubis** was the jackal-headed Egyptian god of mummification and protector of tombs. He guided souls into the afterlife. In the \"weighing of the heart,\" he placed a person's heart on a scale against the feather of truth, and a heart as light as the feather meant the person had lived a good life."],
  ["who is set | set egyptian god", "**Set** (Seth) was the Egyptian god of storms, deserts and chaos. He's shown with the head of a mysterious animal with a long curved snout and tall square ears. In myth he was the jealous brother and rival of Osiris and fought Horus for the throne. Yet he also helped Ra by fighting the chaos serpent Apophis."],
  ["who is thoth | thoth egyptian god", "**Thoth** was the Egyptian god of writing, wisdom, the moon and knowledge, usually shown with the head of an **ibis** bird, or sometimes as a baboon. Egyptians believed he invented hieroglyphic writing, and he was the scribe of the gods, recording the result of the weighing of the heart."],
  ["who is bastet | egyptian cat goddess", "**Bastet** was the Egyptian goddess of the home, cats, protection and joy, shown as a cat or a woman with a cat's head. Cats were greatly loved in ancient Egypt, and her city, Bubastis, held huge festivals in her honor."],
  ["who is hathor | who is sekhmet", "**Hathor** was the Egyptian goddess of love, music, dance and joy, often shown as a cow or a woman with cow horns holding a sun disk. **Sekhmet** was a fierce lion-headed goddess of war and healing. In one myth, Hathor transformed into Sekhmet when Ra was angry."],
  ["who is sobek | who is khepri | what is a scarab beetle in egypt", "**Sobek** was the Egyptian crocodile god of the Nile, linked with strength and protection. **Khepri** was the scarab-beetle god of the rising sun; just as a dung beetle rolls a ball, Egyptians imagined Khepri rolling the sun into the sky each morning. Scarab charms were popular good-luck amulets."],
  ["who is maui | maui polynesian demigod", "**Māui** is a trickster hero in the myths of many Polynesian peoples, including Hawaiian and Māori traditions. Stories say he pulled islands out of the ocean with a magic fishhook, slowed down the sun by lassoing it so days would be longer, and brought fire to people."],
  ["who is quetzalcoatl | aztec gods", "**Quetzalcoatl**, the \"Feathered Serpent,\" was a major god of the Aztecs and other peoples of ancient Mexico, linked to wind, learning and creation. Other important Aztec gods included **Huitzilopochtli**, god of the sun and war, and **Tlaloc**, god of rain."],
  ["who is amaterasu | japanese sun goddess", "**Amaterasu** is the sun goddess in Japanese mythology. In a famous myth, she hid in a cave after an argument with her storm-god brother, plunging the world into darkness. The other gods held a noisy party outside with dancing and laughter, and when she peeked out curiously, they coaxed her back, returning light to the world."],
  ["what is a chinese dragon | chinese dragon meaning", "Unlike fire-breathing European dragons, **Chinese dragons** (lóng) are usually wise, lucky and kind. They're long and snake-like with no wings, and are linked with water, rain, rivers and good fortune. They were symbols of the emperor, and dragon dances are performed at Lunar New Year."],
  ["what is a griffin | what is a kraken | mythical creatures", "A **griffin** is a legendary creature with the body of a lion and the head and wings of an eagle, said to guard treasure. The **kraken** is a giant sea monster from Scandinavian folklore that could pull ships under, likely inspired by sightings of real giant squid. Other famous mythical creatures include unicorns, mermaids and dragons."],
  ["how are movies made | stages of making a movie | how is a film made", "Movies are made in three main stages. In **pre-production**, the team writes the script, finds funding, casts actors, plans shots and builds sets. In **production**, they film all the scenes. In **post-production**, editors cut the footage together and add music, sound effects and visual effects before release."],
  ["what does a film director do | what does a movie director do", "The **director** is the creative leader of a movie. They decide how the story should look and feel, guide the actors' performances, choose camera shots with the cinematographer and oversee the editing. They make thousands of decisions to bring the script to life."],
  ["what does a film producer do | what does a movie producer do", "A **producer** organizes and manages the whole movie project. They find the money, hire the director and key crew, keep the schedule and budget on track and solve problems. Producers are involved from the first idea until the film is released."],
  ["what is a screenplay | what does a screenwriter do", "A **screenplay** is the script for a movie, written in a special format with scene headings, action descriptions and dialogue. The **screenwriter** creates it. A common rule of thumb is that one page of screenplay equals about one minute of screen time."],
  ["what is a storyboard | how do you make a storyboard", "A **storyboard** is a series of drawings, like a comic strip, showing each shot of a film or animation in order before it's made. It helps the team plan camera angles, action and timing. To make one, draw a row of boxes, sketch each shot simply and write notes about action and dialogue underneath."],
  ["what does a cinematographer do | what is a director of photography", "The **cinematographer**, or **director of photography (DP)**, is in charge of how the film looks through the camera. They choose cameras, lenses, lighting and framing, and work with the director to create each shot's mood."],
  ["what does a film editor do | what is a montage in film", "A **film editor** selects the best takes and joins shots together to tell the story, controlling the pace and rhythm of the movie. A **montage** is a series of short shots edited together to show a lot happening quickly, like a training sequence or the passing of time."],
  ["what are the different movie genres | types of movies", "Common movie genres include **action**, **adventure**, **comedy**, **drama**, **fantasy**, **science fiction**, **horror**, **mystery**, **thriller**, **romance**, **musical**, **western**, **animation**, **documentary** and **family** films. Many movies mix genres, like an action-comedy."],
  ["what is a documentary | documentary vs fiction film", "A **documentary** is a nonfiction film about real people, events, nature or ideas, using real footage, interviews and narration. Nature documentaries, history films and biographies are all types. Fiction films, by contrast, tell made-up stories with actors."],
  ["what is a western movie | what is film noir", "A **western** is a genre set in the American Old West of the late 1800s, with cowboys, horses, frontier towns and showdowns. **Film noir** (\"black film\" in French) describes moody crime dramas of the 1940s and 1950s, usually in black and white, with shadowy lighting, detectives and mysterious characters."],
  ["what is a silent film | why were old movies silent", "**Silent films** had moving pictures but no recorded speech, because the technology to synchronize sound with film wasn't practical until the late 1920s. Dialogue appeared on **title cards** between scenes, and theaters had live music from a pianist, organist or even an orchestra. Actors used big expressions and gestures to tell the story."],
  ["when were movies invented | who invented movies", "Many inventors helped create motion pictures in the late 1800s. Thomas Edison's company made the **Kinetoscope**, a peephole viewer for one person. In 1895 in Paris, the **Lumière brothers** held one of the first public film screenings for a paying audience with their Cinématographe, showing short scenes like workers leaving a factory."],
  ["what was the first movie with sound | what is a talkie", "***The Jazz Singer*** (1927) is famous as the first feature-length film with synchronized spoken dialogue and singing. Films with sound were nicknamed **\"talkies,\"** and within a few years nearly all movies had sound, ending the silent era."],
  ["when did movies get color | what is technicolor", "Early films were sometimes colored by hand or tinted, but **Technicolor**, a process that captured color on film, became widely used in the 1930s. *The Wizard of Oz* (1939) famously switches from sepia-toned Kansas to the brilliant colors of Oz. Most movies were in color by the 1960s."],
  ["what is cgi | what is computer generated imagery", "**CGI** (computer-generated imagery) means images created with computers, from digital creatures and explosions to entire worlds. Artists build 3D models, add textures, animate them, light them and **render** them into final frames. CGI can take powerful computers hours to render a single frame."],
  ["what is a green screen | how does green screen work", "A **green screen** (or chroma key) is a bright green background behind actors. Computers remove that exact color and replace it with any other image, like a city, a storm or outer space. Green is used because it's far from human skin tones; blue screens work the same way."],
  ["what are practical effects | what is animatronics", "**Practical effects** are created physically on set instead of with computers, such as models, makeup, fake rain, explosions and puppets. **Animatronics** are robotic puppets with motors inside that make them move realistically, like creatures and dinosaurs in classic films."],
  ["what is motion capture | how does motion capture work", "**Motion capture** (mo-cap) records an actor's real movements so a digital character can move the same way. Actors wear suits covered with markers or sensors, and cameras track them. Facial capture records tiny expressions, so CGI creatures and characters can perform with real human emotion."],
  ["what is a stunt double | what does a stunt performer do", "A **stunt double** is a specially trained performer who does dangerous or difficult action scenes, like falls, fights and car chases, in place of the main actor. Stunts are carefully planned and rehearsed with safety equipment like harnesses, pads and air bags."],
  ["what is foley | how are movie sound effects made", "**Foley** is the art of recording everyday sound effects for movies in a studio, in sync with the picture. Foley artists walk in different shoes for footsteps, snap celery for crunchy sounds and shake gloves for flapping bird wings. It's named after **Jack Foley**, a sound pioneer at Universal Studios."],
  ["what is a film score | soundtrack vs score", "A **film score** is the original instrumental music composed specially for a movie to build mood and emotion, often played by an orchestra. A **soundtrack** usually means all the music in a film, including songs, and also the album released with it."],
  ["what is a sequel | what is a prequel | what is a remake", "A **sequel** continues the story after the first film. A **prequel** tells what happened before it. A **remake** is a new version of an older movie, and a **spin-off** follows a side character or part of the same world. A **reboot** starts a series over from the beginning."],
  ["what is a cameo | what is a cameo appearance", "A **cameo** is a brief appearance by a famous person, often playing themselves or a tiny role, which fans enjoy spotting. Director Alfred Hitchcock famously made a quick cameo in most of his own films."],
  ["what is a blooper | what is a blooper reel", "A **blooper** is a funny mistake made during filming, like an actor forgetting a line, tripping or laughing in the middle of a scene. A **blooper reel** (gag reel) is a collection of these outtakes, sometimes shown during a movie's end credits."],
  ["what is a movie trailer | why is it called a trailer", "A **trailer** is a short preview advertising an upcoming movie. They're called trailers because long ago they were shown *after* the main film, trailing it. Audiences often walked out before seeing them, so theaters moved them to before the movie."],
  ["what are camera shots in film | what is a close up shot | types of camera shots", "Filmmakers use different shots to tell stories. An **extreme long shot** shows a whole landscape, a **long shot** shows a person's full body, a **medium shot** shows them from the waist up and a **close-up** shows just the face to capture emotion. An **over-the-shoulder shot** is common in conversations."],
  ["what is a pan shot | what is a tilt shot | camera movements in film", "A **pan** turns the camera left or right from a fixed spot, and a **tilt** moves it up or down. A **dolly** or **tracking shot** moves the whole camera along, and a **zoom** changes the lens to make things look closer or farther away. Drone and crane shots float above the action."],
  ["what frame rate are movies | why are movies 24 frames per second", "Most movies are filmed and shown at **24 frames per second**. This standard was set when sound came to film in the late 1920s, as a balance between smooth motion and the cost of film stock. It gives movies their familiar, slightly dreamy look."],
  ["what is a fade in film | what is a cut in film | film transitions", "A **cut** is an instant change from one shot to the next and is the most common transition. A **fade** gradually goes to or from black, often marking the start or end of a scene. A **dissolve** blends one shot into the next, and a **wipe** pushes one image off the screen with another."],
  ["what are the oscars | what are the academy awards", "The **Academy Awards**, nicknamed the **Oscars**, are yearly awards for achievement in filmmaking, given by the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences in the US. The first ceremony was held in 1929. Winners receive a gold-plated statuette of a knight standing on a reel of film."],
  ["what is hollywood | what is bollywood | what is nollywood", "**Hollywood** is a district of Los Angeles, California, and the historic center of the American film industry, with its famous hillside sign. **Bollywood** is the nickname for the Hindi-language film industry based in Mumbai, India, known for colorful song-and-dance numbers. **Nollywood** is Nigeria's booming film industry, one of the largest in the world by number of films."],
  ["what is anime | anime vs cartoons", "**Anime** is animation from Japan, known for expressive characters with large eyes, dramatic action and stories in every genre, from comedy to sci-fi. It's often based on **manga** (Japanese comics). Famous anime studios include Studio Ghibli, known for films like *My Neighbor Totoro* and *Spirited Away*."],
  ["what are the parts of a stage | what is upstage and downstage", "Stage directions are given from the actor's point of view facing the audience. **Downstage** is the front, nearest the audience, and **upstage** is the back. The terms come from old theaters where the stage sloped upward toward the back, so actors literally walked \"up\" the stage. **Center stage** is the middle."],
  ["what is stage left and stage right", "**Stage left** and **stage right** are the actor's left and right when facing the audience. So for the audience, stage left appears on their right! The audience's view is called **house left** and **house right**."],
  ["what is a proscenium stage | what is a thrust stage | what is theater in the round", "A **proscenium stage** has a big arch framing the stage like a picture, with the audience in front. A **thrust stage** sticks out into the audience, who sit on three sides. In **theater in the round** (arena stage), the audience surrounds the stage on all sides."],
  ["what is the fourth wall | what does breaking the fourth wall mean", "The **fourth wall** is the imaginary wall between the performers and the audience: a stage set has three walls, and the audience watches through the invisible fourth. **Breaking the fourth wall** is when a character talks directly to the audience or shows they know they're in a show."],
  ["what is a monologue | what is a soliloquy | what is an aside", "A **monologue** is a long speech by one character to others. A **soliloquy** is when a character speaks their private thoughts aloud while alone on stage, like Hamlet's \"To be, or not to be.\" An **aside** is a short comment to the audience that the other characters supposedly can't hear."],
  ["what are the wings in a theater | what is backstage", "The **wings** are the hidden areas at the sides of the stage where actors wait for their entrances and scenery is stored. **Backstage** means all the areas behind and beside the stage that the audience doesn't see, including dressing rooms and the green room, where actors relax."],
  ["what does a stage manager do", "The **stage manager** runs the show once it opens. During rehearsals they keep track of blocking, schedules and notes, and during performances they \"call the show,\" telling the crew exactly when to change lights, sound and scenery. They keep everything running smoothly and safely."],
  ["what is a dress rehearsal | what is a tech rehearsal", "A **tech rehearsal** focuses on the technical side, practicing lighting, sound, set changes and special effects with the actors. A **dress rehearsal** is a full run-through just like a real performance, with costumes, makeup and everything, usually right before opening night."],
  ["why do actors say break a leg", "**\"Break a leg\"** means \"good luck\" in theater. Many performers believe saying \"good luck\" directly is unlucky, so they say the opposite as a playful superstition. Nobody knows its exact origin, but one idea links it to bowing, or \"breaking\" the line of the leg, after a great show."],
  ["what is an understudy | what is a swing in theater", "An **understudy** learns a main actor's role so they can step in if that actor is sick or can't perform. A **swing** is a performer who learns several ensemble parts and can fill in for any of them. Some understudies have become stars after a lucky night on stage!"],
  ["what are props in theater | what is a set | what are costumes", "**Props** (short for properties) are objects actors handle on stage, like cups, swords or letters. The **set** is the scenery that shows where the story takes place. **Costumes** are the clothes actors wear to show who their characters are, and the time and place of the story."],
  ["what is blocking in theater | what is choreography", "**Blocking** is the planned movement and positions of actors on stage, worked out by the director so everyone can be seen and the story is clear. **Choreography** is the planned movement in dance numbers, created by a choreographer."],
  ["what is improv | what does ad lib mean", "**Improv** (improvisation) is acting without a script, making up scenes on the spot, often from audience suggestions. A key rule is **\"Yes, and...\"**: accept what your partner offers and add to it. To **ad-lib** means to make up lines in the moment, especially when something goes wrong."],
  ["what is a curtain call | what is an encore", "A **curtain call** is when performers come back on stage at the end of a show to bow while the audience applauds. An **encore** is an extra performance after the show, usually at a concert, when the audience keeps cheering for more. \"Encore\" is French for \"again.\""],
  ["what is a matinee | what is an intermission", "A **matinee** is a daytime performance of a show or movie, usually in the afternoon. An **intermission** is a break in the middle of a longer show, often 15 to 20 minutes, when the audience can stretch and the crew can change sets."],
  ["what is an act and a scene in a play", "Plays are divided into **acts**, the big sections of the story, and each act is split into smaller **scenes**, which usually change when the time or place changes. Shakespeare's plays have five acts, while many modern plays and musicals have two."],
  ["what does a playwright do | what is a script", "A **playwright** is a person who writes plays. The **script** is the written text, containing the dialogue and **stage directions**, usually in italics, telling actors how to move or speak. The word ends in \"wright,\" meaning a maker, like a shipwright, not \"write.\""],
  ["what is an audition | how to prepare for an audition", "An **audition** is a short tryout where performers show their skills so the director can cast roles. To prepare, learn your monologue or song well, practice in front of someone, speak clearly and slowly, and bring energy. Directors also look for people who are friendly and take direction well."],
  ["what is mime | what is pantomime", "**Mime** is performing a story using only gestures, facial expressions and body movement, with no words, like pretending to be trapped in an invisible box. In Britain, a **pantomime** is a funny family Christmas show based on a fairy tale, with songs, slapstick and the audience shouting \"He's behind you!\""],
  ["what are the types of puppets | what is a marionette", "Common puppets include **hand puppets** (worn like a glove), **finger puppets**, **rod puppets** (moved with sticks), **marionettes** (hung from strings and controlled from above), **shadow puppets** (flat figures casting shadows on a screen) and big **bunraku**-style puppets from Japan, worked by several puppeteers at once."],
  ["what is shadow puppetry | what is wayang kulit", "**Shadow puppetry** uses flat cut-out figures held between a light and a screen, so the audience sees their shadows. It's an ancient art in China, India, Turkey and Southeast Asia. In Indonesia, **wayang kulit** uses intricate leather puppets and gamelan music to tell epic stories through the night."],
  ["what was ancient greek theater like | why are comedy and tragedy masks the symbol of theater", "Ancient Greek plays were performed outdoors in huge hillside theaters, at festivals honoring the god Dionysus. All the actors were men who wore **masks** to show different characters and emotions, and a **chorus** commented on the action. The smiling and frowning masks of **comedy and tragedy** became the symbol of theater."],
  ["what is commedia dell'arte", "**Commedia dell'arte** was a popular form of Italian street theater from the 1500s to 1700s. Traveling actors improvised funny plots using stock characters with masks, like the clever servant **Harlequin** and the grumpy old man Pantalone. It influenced comedy, clowning and pantomime for centuries."],
  ["what is the globe theatre | what was shakespeare's theater", "The **Globe Theatre** was the London playhouse where many of Shakespeare's plays were first performed. Built in 1599, it was round and open to the sky, and poorer audience members called \"groundlings\" stood in the yard. It burned down in 1613 when a stage cannon set the roof alight, and a reconstruction opened near the original site in 1997."],
  ["what is kabuki | what is noh theater", "**Kabuki** is a lively Japanese theater style that began in the early 1600s, known for bold makeup, dazzling costumes, dramatic poses and special effects like trapdoors and quick costume changes. **Noh**, from the 1300s, is much older and slower, with masked performers, chanting and graceful, symbolic movement."],
  ["what is ballet | what are the five ballet positions", "**Ballet** is a graceful form of dance that started in the royal courts of Renaissance Italy and France. Dancers learn **five basic positions** of the feet, all with legs turned out from the hips. Many ballet terms are French, like *plié* (bend) and *pirouette* (spin), and ballerinas often dance on the tips of their toes in pointe shoes."],
  ["what are famous ballets | what is swan lake about", "Famous ballets include ***Swan Lake***, ***The Nutcracker*** and ***The Sleeping Beauty***, all with music by Tchaikovsky, plus *Giselle*, *Coppélia* and *Romeo and Juliet*. In *Swan Lake*, Prince Siegfried falls in love with Odette, a princess turned into a swan by a sorcerer's spell that only true love can break."],
  ["what is a spotlight | how does stage lighting work", "Stage lighting helps the audience see and sets the mood. A **spotlight** is a strong beam that follows a performer, often run by an operator at the back of the theater. Colored filters called **gels** tint the light, and a lighting designer programs a control board to change the lights for every moment of the show."]
);
